WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.040
My name is Shannon
Rose McAuliffe

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.470
and I'm the Manager of
the Student Arts Programs

00:00:04.470 --> 00:00:05.790
here at MIT.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.070
On behalf of the entire
Office of the Arts,

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.650
welcome and thank you all
so much for joining us

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:14.440
in our newly virtual 15K
Creative Arts Competition

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.430
Pitch Finale.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:18.350
Tonight is our
Eighth Annual Pitch

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:21.050
Finale for the 15K Competition.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:24.920
Since 2013, The 15K has
served as an incubator

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.560
for creative
entrepreneurial startups

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.840
and has helped dozens
of student teams

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.080
grow their ideas into
influential businesses

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.010
at the intersection of
arts and innovation.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.070
We had 17 creative
entrepreneurship ventures

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:42.650
applied to the
competition this year.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.600
Throughout the entire
process, the teams

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.690
have demonstrated truly
impressive improvement

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.560
and a remarkably collegial,
collaborative spirit.

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.090
All of the teams
are to be commended

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.180
for their flexibility,
perseverance, and integrity

00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:00.660
in the midst of so much
unexpected upheaval.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:03.630
Following a round of
judging, six finalists

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:08.280
have emerged for the chance to
win $15,000 towards supporting

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.720
their projects.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:11.850
It's been a privilege
to witness the growth

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:14.370
that each team has
undergone on their journey

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:16.450
here this afternoon.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.790
This competition was
designed to provide benefit

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:21.430
to creative ventures
at all levels,

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.970
and it's important to note
that all 17 initial teams were

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.390
offered the opportunity
to experience the three

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.250
key aspects of the 15K.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:38.020
The first is Idea
Development and Workshops.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.240
Each team received
thorough feedback

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.320
from seasoned arts professionals
on their initial applications,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.190
which helped the teams to hone
and clarify their projects.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.610
Teams also attended a
series of targeted workshops

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.300
with experts in the area of
creative entrepreneurship,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:58.780
designed to accelerate all
ventures during the competition

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.190
period.

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:02.410
These workshops were
tailored toward topics

00:02:02.410 --> 00:02:05.440
such as value propositions,
long-term business

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.130
sustainability, and perfecting
the art of storytelling

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:10.119
through the live pitch.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.740
Through these
experiences, our teams

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:18.380
have grown their ideas
and their business savvy

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.200
on a broad scale,
and these workshops

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.750
are augmented by more granular
individual mentorship.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:29.110
All participants experience
one-on-one mentorship sessions

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:32.560
with distinguished MIT
faculty and alumni,

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.710
creative entrepreneurs,
artists, attorneys,

00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:39.520
marketing specialists, and
industry leaders, all of whom

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.820
provide nuanced insight
and expertise into helping

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:46.180
teams develop their projects.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.220
And all of this happens through
a platform called Mentorly.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.230
The 15K and Mentorly have
been working together

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:55.540
for three years
now, and the role

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:58.030
they played this year was
instrumental in pairing

00:02:58.030 --> 00:03:01.390
our teams with suggested
mentors, data and schedule

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:05.140
management, and stewarding the
mentorship process for everyone

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.580
involved.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:09.370
The teams cumulatively
undertook 39

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:12.580
independent one-on-one
mentoring sessions, in addition

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.030
to the aforementioned rounds
of feedback, workshops,

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.940
and mock pitch events.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:22.360
The third, integral element
of the 15K Competition

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.900
comes down to a single word,
and that's connections.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:27.700
Our student teams
make connections

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.130
with other students,
with alumni,

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:31.990
and with industry mentors.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:35.170
This network continues to
bolster and support them,

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:38.770
even after graduation, as they
seek opportunities to bring

00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:41.310
their ideas to fruition.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.400
And this year's process,
culminating in today's finale,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.140
would not have been possible
without the expertise,

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:52.000
guidance, and support
from the MIT community.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.580
I'd like to take a moment
and extend special thanks

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:57.430
to our mentors and first
round judges who volunteered

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.640
their time,
knowledge, resources,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.435
and insightful expertise
throughout this process.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:09.170
We've also got our workshop
leaders from Thought Café,

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:13.550
Creative Re/Frame, Spaceus,
and our mock pitch panel.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.800
These people all
spent hours talking

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.980
to these teams about their
entrepreneurial experiences

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:22.790
and sharing information
with our teams.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:25.160
Thank you also to
Mentorly for providing us

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.710
with constant support
and an online platform

00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:31.730
to facilitate and track
our mentoring sessions.

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.430
I also owe a tremendous
debt of gratitude

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.800
to my inimitable
colleagues in the MIT

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:39.980
Office for the Arts for
their insights, support,

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.810
and for assisting
me with turning this

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.270
into a virtual event.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:45.830
This wouldn't have been
possible without them.

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:49.640
And I'd also like to give a
huge thanks to Teach to Learn,

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:52.820
who were the 2019
winners of the 15K

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:54.650
and they'll be providing
an update tonight

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:57.750
while our judges deliberate.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:00.570
And on the topic of
judges, enthusiastic

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:03.930
thanks to our estimable
panel of finalist judges

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:09.170
including Josh Roy, Wendy Swart
Grossman, Phillip Tiongson, Gui

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:13.710
Marcondes, Mary Hale, Eran
Egozy, and Mark Steglitz,

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:19.410
who have been and continue to be
champions of this competition.

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.730
So before we commence
with the pitches,

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:23.920
I'm going to go over
a bit of housekeeping.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.250
For attendees, competitors,
and judges alike,

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:30.700
please keep your audio muted
and keep your camera off

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.570
unless you are speaking.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.630
Each team will have five
minutes to pitch to the panel,

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:39.120
five minutes for
questions from judges,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:41.550
and one minute for transition.

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.860
When there are 60 seconds
left on the timer,

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.170
you will hear a dinging sound.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:50.310
And after the 60
seconds has lapsed,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.920
you will hear the
dinging sound replayed.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.800
And we're going to endeavor
to stick it to this timing

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:59.250
schedule as closely as we can.

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.290
After the six
pitches, judges will

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.680
exit for a 45-minute
deliberation period

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.280
where you'll have an opportunity
to ask questions of our teams,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.980
participate in
audience choice voting,

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:15.030
and to hear an update from our
2019 winner, Teach to Learn.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.850
If you have any questions for
the teams throughout the event,

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:20.280
please submit them
via the Q&amp;A button

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.960
at the bottom of your screen.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.750
And now we're going to
do a quick roll call.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.700
Teams, please come
on camera and give us

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.320
a wave when you're called.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:35.020
First up tonight, we have
Jeremy and David with Rarify.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.630
Then we have Alan and
Derek with ArcLive.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:45.450
Then we've got Carolyn and
Natasha with P(art)ner.

00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:50.540
Following that, we have Michael
and Maria with ELEMENTS.

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.920
Then we'll have Joe and Iris
with Material Futures Lab,

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:59.370
and we have Piyush
representing UnMake.

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:01.500
Now I wish all of
the competitors

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:05.790
good luck and good skill,
and let's start pitching.

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:07.470
Team Rarify, you're up!

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.330
All right, so hello everyone.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.460
My name is David Rosenwasser.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:18.490
And I'm Jeremy Bilotti.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.860
We are excited to tell
you about our new design

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.260
brand called Rarify.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:25.700
So to give you
some background, I

00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:28.640
started collecting and restoring
vintage designer furniture

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:30.750
as a high school student
by flipping chairs

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.060
from Craigslist.

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.060
Just as I was worrying about how
to pay for architecture school,

00:07:35.060 --> 00:07:37.640
a store owner in the
Philippines saw what I was doing

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.620
and decided to acquire
all of the inventory

00:07:39.620 --> 00:07:41.810
that I had at the time.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.090
That let me start a real
business while helping

00:07:44.090 --> 00:07:46.800
to pay for college.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.560
Fast-forward to today,
I've spent nearly 10 years

00:07:49.560 --> 00:07:50.595
running D ROSE MOD.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.780
During this time, I
have restored hundreds

00:07:55.780 --> 00:07:58.240
of iconic pieces of
furniture, spent thousands

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:01.210
of hours researching,
and changed hands

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:04.630
with over 5,000 pieces.

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:06.490
So David and I go way back.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.530
In fact, he was actually
the first person

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:10.630
that I met at
architecture school.

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:13.720
So as we both studied and
David grew his business,

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.750
I was becoming fascinated with
the intersections of design

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:18.460
and technology.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:20.770
I ended up working at
interdisciplinary design

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:23.020
studios, then at the
Self-Assembly Lab

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:26.350
here at MIT, and with
Microsoft Research.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:29.200
I became fascinated with how
machine learning and data

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.370
science will transform the
way we interact with design.

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:35.500
Now, David and I
are ready to disrupt

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:40.260
the furniture-buying experience
using cutting-edge technology.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.770
So the designer
furniture market today

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:44.640
is worth over $25 billion.

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:49.130
However, there are a number
of issues facing collectors.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.010
When looking to buy authentic
vintage or new designer

00:08:52.010 --> 00:08:54.920
furniture, it's really hard for
consumers to know what's real

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:59.370
and what's fake, what's a good
investment and what is not.

00:08:59.370 --> 00:09:01.140
Brands that consumers
have come to trust,

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.160
like CB2, Restoration
Hardware, and Room &amp; Board,

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:07.110
have been capitalizing off
of knockoffs for decades,

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.810
even with high prices.

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:12.810
Let's look at Sam and
Alex, for instance.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:16.470
Sam bought a high-quality
vintage Barcelona chair for 3K

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:19.800
and Alex spent the same
money on a fake one.

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.710
Years later, Sam still
has a fantastic investment

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:25.890
whereas Alex's chair is hard
to sell and has about 10%

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:28.440
of its original value.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:29.730
That's right, David.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:32.130
So collectors of the
future want to know

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:34.530
that they're investing
in real value,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:36.870
and that value needs
to be verified.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.360
They're more informed than
ever, so they need access

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.570
to the most collectable
pieces out there.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:46.590
That's why we founded Rarify
on the values of transparency

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:48.790
and quality.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.640
Our mission is to make the
furniture-buying experience

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:55.660
more transparent and
informative using big data.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.650
So here's how we'll do it.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:00.800
First, Rarify carries
collectibles of the past,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.570
present and future.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:04.580
We collaborate with
emerging designers

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:07.850
to make new furniture,
alongside our recent and vintage

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.740
inventory.

00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:13.520
Next, we show our customers
the authenticity and the value

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:17.180
of each vintage piece,
verified by experts and machine

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:18.730
learning.

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:21.250
Lastly, we sell
direct-to-consumer.

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:24.460
We leverage our existing
supply chain of fabricators,

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:27.530
craftspeople, and shippers.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.330
We have over a
decade of experience

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:32.420
bringing investment-worthy
furniture to collectors.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:36.020
And now, we're launching as
the design and tech company.

00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:37.910
We use data science
to bring collectors

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:41.330
more information, more
clarity, and more confidence.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.180
So how exactly
does our tech work?

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:48.540
Well, our proprietary
authentication technology

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.210
uses machine learning
to verify details

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:54.550
about each piece,
such as this chair.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:57.610
So this means material,
form, condition,

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:59.470
the presence of labels.

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:02.620
It also assesses the piece's
investment potential,

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.450
based on things
like market prices

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:07.880
and even the designer's
social media presence.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.400
Finally, it assigns
it a score which

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:15.540
collectors can see alongside
a verified expert score.

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:17.730
Our authentication
technology is going

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:22.520
to revolutionize the way
collectors buy rare furniture.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:23.210
Thanks, Jeremy.

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:25.580
So what does the e-commerce
furniture landscape

00:11:25.580 --> 00:11:26.750
look like today?

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:29.990
There are a lot of ways to buy
a designer furniture online,

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:32.930
and the fastest growing segment
is high-end designer retail,

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:37.630
which includes vintage according
to the US Chamber of Commerce.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.240
Now there are two primary
competitors, both of which

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.220
are growing rapidly.

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:45.460
1stdibs sells high-dollar
items within a luxury market,

00:11:45.460 --> 00:11:48.070
whereas Chairish is more
oriented toward décor

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:50.796
and lower-value
items in comparison.

00:11:50.796 --> 00:11:53.520
Rarify is a disruptor,
setting a standard

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:55.980
for authentication
and transparency

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:58.410
unlike the competition.

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:02.190
Now, part of the market growth
is because there's $350 billion

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:04.770
in high-end collectibles.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:07.710
Now our business model relies
on commissions from our partner

00:12:07.710 --> 00:12:11.580
dealers like D ROSE MOD,
Rarify's in-house inventory,

00:12:11.580 --> 00:12:15.030
new designer collaborations, and
revenue from our authentication

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:16.390
technology.

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:19.830
And opportunity from 15K will
provide critical rocket fuel

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.920
allowing us to further
develop our back-end tech,

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:25.960
and our first designer
collaboration.

00:12:25.960 --> 00:12:28.310
Lastly, with our skills
complementing one another

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:30.440
and our passions going
in the same direction,

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:32.390
we are ready to grow
Rarify into something

00:12:32.390 --> 00:12:35.380
that is truly special.

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:36.920
Thank you so much.

00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:37.420
Thank you.

00:12:40.280 --> 00:12:41.930
Thank you so much!

00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:45.005
And now, we'll have some
Q&amp;A from our judges.

00:12:48.530 --> 00:12:50.790
Mark.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:55.480
The vintage purchasing of
furniture for collection--

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:56.960
a significant part
of the market?

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.660
Or do people just
buy furniture for use

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:01.850
including vintage furniture?

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:04.050
So, we're trying to look at--

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:05.820
so I have a pretty
extensive background

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:07.955
in dealing with this
furniture and the reality

00:13:07.955 --> 00:13:09.330
is that for many
of these pieces,

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:10.997
they are indeed
investments, that people

00:13:10.997 --> 00:13:14.070
look at as both something that
they can look at everyday,

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:16.888
but also as something that's a
collectible piece that someone

00:13:16.888 --> 00:13:18.180
is concerned about their value.

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.700
And I have customers
pretty frequently

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.000
who ask what kind of
Eames lounge chairs

00:13:23.000 --> 00:13:26.100
should they buy based upon
collectability and rarity.

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.530
And so that's something that
we're hugely attentive to, as

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:31.320
well as applying those
ideas to the new collections

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.740
and how we engage collectible
designers in new collections

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:35.328
as well.

00:13:38.316 --> 00:13:39.810
Wonderful.

00:13:39.810 --> 00:13:40.310
Phillip.

00:13:44.820 --> 00:13:46.760
Thanks.

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:48.330
For me, what's
interesting about this

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:52.430
is this idea of an
authentication tech,

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:56.190
that there is some way that
through machine learning

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:59.850
you can create something
that couldn't be fooled.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:03.750
So a lot of that has
to do with learning--

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:06.630
having a lot of access to the
data about the original stuff,

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:11.820
as well as, how does anyone
trust your authentication?

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.530
If there's one thing
I do know, there's

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:20.820
ways to fool verification,
facial recognition.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:25.350
And so, if people were
able to study your process,

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:29.980
couldn't they just find
a way to fool your tech?

00:14:29.980 --> 00:14:32.972
Yeah, I think that's
a really good point.

00:14:32.972 --> 00:14:34.680
I mean, part of our
mission is definitely

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.150
to promote transparency
and expose all of the data,

00:14:39.150 --> 00:14:41.280
perhaps, that we
use in a way that

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:44.010
helps give people confidence.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.230
Obviously, there's a
limit to that transparency

00:14:46.230 --> 00:14:48.310
because it is
intellectual property

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:51.930
and we want to preserve that
as part of our business.

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:56.190
But on the consumer end,
we will be prioritizing

00:14:56.190 --> 00:14:59.520
sharing which features
we're looking at, and also

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:02.730
documenting each piece
to a higher standard

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:04.810
than what's on the market today.

00:15:04.810 --> 00:15:07.620
So you might be able to see the
piece that you're looking at,

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:12.030
identify every scratch, spin
it in 3D on the website,

00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:15.060
and also understand the
features and the decisions that

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:18.240
went into scoring that piece.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:20.640
How we can keep it
private and protect

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:24.420
our IP is that the data
set that we're using

00:15:24.420 --> 00:15:26.080
is actually one that
we already have.

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:28.670
So we have a whole
collection of images

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:31.980
and labeled data that we
can work with already,

00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:34.820
and start training our
models on right away.

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:37.770
And that's all taken
by us, and by David,

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:40.890
over the course of his
tenure running D ROSE MOD.

00:15:45.220 --> 00:15:46.445
All right.

00:15:46.445 --> 00:15:46.945
Josh.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:55.800
So I see that a portion of
your business, while some of it

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:58.830
is existing furniture, you
have an interesting side

00:15:58.830 --> 00:16:02.130
of this business that's tied
to designer partnerships

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.480
and collaborations.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.862
Can you speak a little bit
more about your arrangements

00:16:06.862 --> 00:16:08.820
with those designers,
how you're sourcing them,

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.278
how they're compensated, and
what that side of the business

00:16:11.278 --> 00:16:12.180
looks like?

00:16:12.180 --> 00:16:12.810
Yes.

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.180
So we have not-- to be
clear, we have not formally

00:16:15.180 --> 00:16:17.420
brought on our first
designer collaboration

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:18.630
yet for new pieces.

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:21.990
But the idea is that, as far
as the general resources,

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:26.310
we have an existing supply
chain of shippers, fabricators,

00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:28.020
warehouse storage,
et cetera, that

00:16:28.020 --> 00:16:32.205
exists utilizing the resources
from the existing business.

00:16:32.205 --> 00:16:34.080
And then as far as
bringing on new designers,

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:36.090
the main initiative we
want to bring forward

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:39.930
is representing both an
up-and-coming and desirable

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:43.350
group of new designers, but also
a diverse range of designers.

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:45.630
So the idea is, how do
we bring aboard people

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:48.450
who we feel like will be
developing collectible pieces

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:49.530
of the future?

00:16:49.530 --> 00:16:51.720
And we will use our
own in-house criteria

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.630
and also make sure
that the royalties are

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.510
ideally better than what is
typically offered in industry.

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:00.270
And we understand that that's
an issue facing designers today

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:01.586
as well.

00:17:01.586 --> 00:17:04.140
Yeah, and what's really
interesting there also,

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:08.970
is that we can have full
control over these pieces

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:13.540
and document them
extremely thoroughly.

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:16.109
So we can kind of communicate
how they're authentic,

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:18.150
how they're rare, and
how they're unique,

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.369
and then using the
data that we've

00:17:20.369 --> 00:17:22.500
trained our machine
learning model on,

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:25.470
we can also come up with the
score for these new pieces

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:28.770
and anticipate that they might
have a particular investment

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:30.235
value for someone.

00:17:30.235 --> 00:17:33.625
Yeah, and the analogy today
is, we're looking at the idea

00:17:33.625 --> 00:17:36.000
if you were to build a company
like Noel or Herman Miller

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.333
today, what would they have
done and how would they have

00:17:38.333 --> 00:17:39.640
developed those relationships.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:42.360
So we're looking at innovative
up-and-coming designers,

00:17:42.360 --> 00:17:44.610
but also embedding
technology, trying

00:17:44.610 --> 00:17:47.910
to make that streamlined for
consumers direct-to-consumer,

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.448
and also using
in-house fabrication

00:17:50.448 --> 00:17:51.990
and fabricators so
that we don't need

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:55.360
to charge for third-party
fabricators and so forth.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:17:59.580
Wonderful.

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:01.440
And I believe we have
time for one more.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:03.510
Let's have Wendy.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:05.670
Though I just heard
the 60 second ding,

00:18:05.670 --> 00:18:08.638
so you have one minute.

00:18:08.638 --> 00:18:10.820
First, David, Jeremy, so fun.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:12.500
So fun to see how it's all gone.

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:13.850
Thank you.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:16.010
Quick question for you.

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:18.423
I'm curious-- first
off, Roadside Antiques.

00:18:18.423 --> 00:18:20.840
I think you guys need to be
on there, that would be really

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:22.640
great to get them as a client.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:26.710
I'm curious about though,
the monetization of this.

00:18:26.710 --> 00:18:28.928
Who's going to be
your first customer

00:18:28.928 --> 00:18:30.220
and what are they going to pay?

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:33.170
Is it an ongoing
subscription, what's the deal?

00:18:33.170 --> 00:18:35.330
OK, so there are a few
different business models.

00:18:35.330 --> 00:18:37.160
The first one,
straightforward, is

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.230
we will be approaching
this as a business

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:41.970
and rigorously going through
advertising, social media.

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:43.850
And so a lot of
the initial revenue

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:47.840
will be coming from taking
a commission of the existing

00:18:47.840 --> 00:18:49.400
sales from the
previous business,

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:53.900
and that will help fuel both,
along with hopefully with 15K,

00:18:53.900 --> 00:18:55.620
the new designer collaborations.

00:18:55.620 --> 00:18:57.920
So the initial will
be the vintage pieces,

00:18:57.920 --> 00:19:00.248
and then immediately we'll
start bringing in designers,

00:19:00.248 --> 00:19:01.790
bringing about
collaborations, and we

00:19:01.790 --> 00:19:04.340
hope within the first
one to two years,

00:19:04.340 --> 00:19:06.590
the majority of our business
will then transition over

00:19:06.590 --> 00:19:08.150
to the new
collections so that we

00:19:08.150 --> 00:19:12.950
can focus on emerging
designers and new, rare pieces.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:13.770
OK.

00:19:13.770 --> 00:19:14.270
Great.

00:19:14.270 --> 00:19:15.200
Wonderful.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:16.580
Thank you all so much.

00:19:16.580 --> 00:19:18.890
Thank you to Team Rarify.

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:22.340
Up next, we've got ArcLive.

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:24.380
Thank you everybody.

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:25.080
Hello everyone.

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:26.538
My name's Alan,
and I'm going to be

00:19:26.538 --> 00:19:27.830
talking to you about ArcLive.

00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:30.560
So as a quick
introduction, ArcLive

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.840
is a video conferencing
research tool

00:19:32.840 --> 00:19:36.350
that helps historians conduct
their research internationally

00:19:36.350 --> 00:19:40.080
by providing live access
to non-digitized documents.

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:43.340
So we're basically like Doctor
on Demand, but for research.

00:19:43.340 --> 00:19:46.820
So most documents aren't online.

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:49.490
And if you really want
to learn about who

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:52.100
an artist was, what
they were like,

00:19:52.100 --> 00:19:54.740
you'll look at their notes.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.170
On the left here, we have
a Vitruvian Man, which

00:19:57.170 --> 00:19:59.630
came from Leonardo's notes.

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:02.435
And usually if you
want to access these,

00:20:02.435 --> 00:20:05.060
you're going to-- it says on the
right there underlined, likely

00:20:05.060 --> 00:20:09.030
that you'll need to visit the
research room to actually see

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:09.530
these notes.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:12.350
Most of them aren't online, and
so your average reading room

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:13.520
looks something like this.

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:17.120
You have hundreds of
thousands of documents stored.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:20.150
This is a repository where
they're readily accessible,

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:22.520
and then long-term they're
put in depositories.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.978
It's kind of like Indiana Jones
and the Ark of the Covenant

00:20:24.978 --> 00:20:27.440
where they wheel the Ark
into that giant storage room.

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:28.550
This is an archive.

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:30.050
This is basically
what they're like.

00:20:30.050 --> 00:20:32.563
And they measure--
usually, they don't even

00:20:32.563 --> 00:20:33.980
know how many
documents there are.

00:20:33.980 --> 00:20:37.010
They measure them in cubic feet,
that's the easiest way to do it

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:39.130
because nobody's ever
counted them all.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:41.320
And just as an industry,
document imaging

00:20:41.320 --> 00:20:43.690
is about a $21 billion market.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:47.380
Our early accessible market,
we estimate it $350 million.

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:49.090
We split that into
two groups, which

00:20:49.090 --> 00:20:53.680
is the Traveling Researchers,
so historians, genealogists.

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:55.580
And then, research firms.

00:20:55.580 --> 00:20:58.510
That's let's say, you just
want to contract someone,

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:01.270
you can hire them from there.

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:04.360
So on average, less than
10% of archival documents

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:05.650
are available online.

00:21:05.650 --> 00:21:08.680
And the reason for that
is usually archives

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:11.200
can't afford the
digitization costs.

00:21:11.200 --> 00:21:13.240
If they're lucky, they'll
get a grant to do it,

00:21:13.240 --> 00:21:16.330
but then they have to manage
those documents forever.

00:21:16.330 --> 00:21:18.605
There's a recurring cost
associated with that.

00:21:18.605 --> 00:21:20.230
But also have to
worry about copyright.

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:23.110
Usually documents acquired
within the last 30 to 40 years,

00:21:23.110 --> 00:21:26.560
they didn't expressly get
those copyright permissions

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.540
to digitally distribute.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:30.700
And so as a result if
somebody wants to research,

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:33.700
it's filled with
risk and uncertainty.

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:35.278
Nowadays with long
flights that you

00:21:35.278 --> 00:21:36.820
have to deal with
to physically visit

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:39.940
the archives, especially
internationally, most archives

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:42.550
have restricted hours so
like 10:00 AM to 4:00 PM.

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:44.287
We actually heard
one researcher who

00:21:44.287 --> 00:21:46.120
was so worried she
wouldn't have enough time

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:49.270
to conduct her research that
she opted to skip lunch for five

00:21:49.270 --> 00:21:53.620
days to maximize her time in
the archive, which is crazy.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:56.930
And then they have to worry
about the copyright, too.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:00.930
And so combined with
the coronavirus now,

00:22:00.930 --> 00:22:04.530
we're starting to see how
valuable online accessibility

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:05.520
tools can be.

00:22:05.520 --> 00:22:08.640
We can never go back to the
way things were before this

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:11.470
and that creates a really
unique opportunity.

00:22:11.470 --> 00:22:13.950
And so we develop a new
protocol that we call

00:22:13.950 --> 00:22:15.240
controlled remote visitation.

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:16.740
This is our
prototype on the left

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:20.520
here, which allows you
to work with a surrogate

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.860
to access documents
in real time,

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:24.990
and this circumnavigates
the copyright restrictions

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:27.120
because this is a
form of live access.

00:22:27.120 --> 00:22:30.835
We aren't making
permanent replications.

00:22:30.835 --> 00:22:31.460
So, what we do.

00:22:31.460 --> 00:22:33.170
Normally, it would
cost about $1,800

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:35.750
to travel, say from to Boston
from the UK, about $60 bucks

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:36.500
an hour.

00:22:36.500 --> 00:22:37.610
We cut that in half.

00:22:37.610 --> 00:22:40.993
We charge $35 per hour for our
service, which you can then

00:22:40.993 --> 00:22:42.410
take your travel
budget and use it

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:45.560
to access different
geographic locations based

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:48.260
on the collections they
have, rather than spending it

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:50.270
all in one place.

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:54.770
So most other companies
focus on the public domain

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:57.350
or the highest-priority
documents that are available

00:22:57.350 --> 00:22:59.060
now, which is about 10%.

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:00.650
We focus on everything else.

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:03.320
Everything that you
want to digitize,

00:23:03.320 --> 00:23:05.090
but it isn't worth
it, or else things

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:08.420
that are just never
going to be digitized.

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:12.350
As far as our technical stack
goes, we have the Hovercam.

00:23:12.350 --> 00:23:14.090
These are the
workstations we issue.

00:23:14.090 --> 00:23:16.520
For our back-end,
we're using vMix CALL,

00:23:16.520 --> 00:23:18.360
is what encodes the video.

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:20.780
And then we distribute it
using Twilio, an online video

00:23:20.780 --> 00:23:21.770
platform.

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:24.680
And we're going to be
using Google AutoML later.

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:27.510
I'll talk a little
bit about that later.

00:23:27.510 --> 00:23:28.010
Excuse me.

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:29.255
How do we prevent screenshots?

00:23:29.255 --> 00:23:30.080
[ONE MINUTE WARNING BELL]
One minute warning.

00:23:30.080 --> 00:23:33.060
The video game industry
solved this a while ago.

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:33.860
Water marking.

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:36.000
Alpha channel layering
is what it's called.

00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:37.950
So we don't have to
worry about that.

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:40.190
We can use this and
have an active ticker

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:42.793
to track who is doing that.

00:23:42.793 --> 00:23:44.210
As far as partnerships,
we already

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:46.168
have our first pilot with
the Thoreau Institute

00:23:46.168 --> 00:23:47.360
at Walden Ponds.

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:49.290
Henry David Thoreau.

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.320
Walden Pond in Mass.

00:23:51.320 --> 00:23:53.300
Long-term, we want
to partner with

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:54.560
several other organizations.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:57.063
And then, as far as
corporate partnerships,

00:23:57.063 --> 00:23:58.730
we're looking at the
Library of Congress

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:02.055
who legislates
copyrights, as well

00:24:02.055 --> 00:24:03.180
as Google and Ancestry.com.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:07.340
Our roadmap for the
next three years--

00:24:07.340 --> 00:24:09.410
pilot in Boston, 10 archives.

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:11.450
Spread throughout
the US in year two,

00:24:11.450 --> 00:24:14.510
and then move
internationally later.

00:24:14.510 --> 00:24:15.380
This is our team.

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:16.940
My name's Alan.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:19.400
Derek is a Sloan
Fellow with MIT.

00:24:19.400 --> 00:24:21.720
I went through the
PlayLabs Accelerator.

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:23.067
And that's ArcLive.

00:24:23.067 --> 00:24:23.900
Thank you very much.

00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.220
Thank you so much, ArcLive.

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:32.330
I'd like to open it up for
Q&amp;A from our judges now.

00:24:37.730 --> 00:24:39.467
Alan, if you could
stop sharing, please.

00:24:39.467 --> 00:24:40.050
Here, hold on.

00:24:40.050 --> 00:24:42.180
Here we go.

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:42.770
Let's see.

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:49.642
Hold on, we can do it.

00:24:49.642 --> 00:24:50.350
I believe in you.

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:54.150
There it is.

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:54.650
Perfect.

00:24:54.650 --> 00:24:55.640
OK.

00:24:55.640 --> 00:24:56.230
All right.

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:01.370
Do we have any
initial questions?

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.440
Mark.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:07.530
I'm a curator, and I'm
interested in a piece of art

00:25:07.530 --> 00:25:11.970
and need to do some
research in the archives.

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.300
Do I have enough information
to tell your research assistant

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:18.630
just to do-- if you excuse the
expression, the grunt work--

00:25:18.630 --> 00:25:20.170
get to the source
of the material

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:22.560
or are you expecting
the researcher

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:25.830
to do a certain amount of
interpretation of his or her

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:28.970
own and formulate conclusions?

00:25:28.970 --> 00:25:31.200
So, good question.

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:33.390
Who's still conducting
the research?

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.650
We function as an
accessibility tool.

00:25:35.650 --> 00:25:39.390
And so the surrogate that
we have onsite, the research

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:41.520
assistant, their
job is to make sure

00:25:41.520 --> 00:25:44.230
that they can
provide safe access.

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:47.920
The researcher themselves,
being the subject matter,

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:50.700
they would be the ones
responsible for interpreting

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:53.280
what they're seeing and
then determining what

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:55.320
to do next with the research.

00:25:55.320 --> 00:25:57.570
So they have to have
enough knowledge

00:25:57.570 --> 00:26:01.390
to point your research assistant
in the right direction.

00:26:01.390 --> 00:26:03.900
Yes, so it would be
up to the researcher

00:26:03.900 --> 00:26:06.630
to request those documents
from storage to be

00:26:06.630 --> 00:26:08.780
pulled into the reading room.

00:26:08.780 --> 00:26:11.540
And then we will help
researchers do that.

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:14.730
We work with the archivist to
pull documents that they need.

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:18.030
And we specifically help
with the discovery process,

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:19.290
of figuring out--

00:26:19.290 --> 00:26:21.912
usually, the discovery process
of going into an archive,

00:26:21.912 --> 00:26:23.370
you're looking for
what sources you

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:29.070
want to use to cite or include
in, say, a historical novel

00:26:29.070 --> 00:26:30.740
or something like that.

00:26:30.740 --> 00:26:33.250
I can maybe give just a
very quick, specific example

00:26:33.250 --> 00:26:34.110
to this.

00:26:34.110 --> 00:26:37.100
My family's really big
into genealogical research,

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:41.340
and we read recently found
a History of the List Family

00:26:41.340 --> 00:26:45.420
at a very small Ross
County Historical Library,

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.160
just outside of Columbus, Ohio.

00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:49.440
The problem is due to
copyright restrictions,

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:52.500
they only have one
physical copy onsite.

00:26:52.500 --> 00:26:54.060
And so the only
way we can access

00:26:54.060 --> 00:26:56.640
that is to physically go there.

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:58.860
And with a tool
like ArcLive, what

00:26:58.860 --> 00:27:01.890
we'd be able to actually do
is call in their one volunteer

00:27:01.890 --> 00:27:04.470
who's there from
8:00 AM to 6:00 PM,

00:27:04.470 --> 00:27:06.480
would be able to actually
open up that resource

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:10.060
and make it available
to us for a paid fee.

00:27:10.060 --> 00:27:12.290
All right, let's
hear from Mary next.

00:27:15.760 --> 00:27:17.390
Hi, thanks.

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:21.260
I was curious how you determine
to pay for the archive

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:26.180
assistant, and if part
of your calculation

00:27:26.180 --> 00:27:28.863
had to do with the
locations of major archives

00:27:28.863 --> 00:27:30.530
that people would be
using, and the cost

00:27:30.530 --> 00:27:32.630
of living in those locations.

00:27:32.630 --> 00:27:33.350
Good question.

00:27:33.350 --> 00:27:37.130
So we average the
pay at $15 per hour.

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:39.248
These are entry-level
research positions,

00:27:39.248 --> 00:27:40.790
so all the money
that would have been

00:27:40.790 --> 00:27:43.730
going to the flights and
hotels is brought back in

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:46.460
to create sustainable
jobs in the industry.

00:27:46.460 --> 00:27:48.875
That may vary over time.

00:27:48.875 --> 00:27:50.750
One of the things that
we're looking at doing

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:54.140
is increasing our prices
from $35 per hour, up to $50

00:27:54.140 --> 00:28:00.020
per hour with add-ons
such as OCR translation,

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:02.720
or OCR tracking, real time
translation, which is better

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:04.400
than doing research in person.

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:07.460
Being able to scan
a page, metadata

00:28:07.460 --> 00:28:10.880
extraction because that's
transformative in nature.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:13.820
We can save metadata, and we'll
just hand that to the archives,

00:28:13.820 --> 00:28:16.190
you know we'll just
give it for free.

00:28:16.190 --> 00:28:17.880
And then keyword extraction.

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:21.360
So if you're
researching John Smith,

00:28:21.360 --> 00:28:23.630
if we find Jonathan
Smith we can say,

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:26.660
hey, this is 92% relevant to
your research topic, which

00:28:26.660 --> 00:28:29.180
is where the Google
AutoML comes in

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.520
and that will allow us
to dramatically increase

00:28:31.520 --> 00:28:34.290
our wages.

00:28:34.290 --> 00:28:36.650
Just to follow up
on that real quick,

00:28:36.650 --> 00:28:38.600
there's no real
one-size-fits-all approach

00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:39.260
here.

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:40.910
So we're going to
have to look closely

00:28:40.910 --> 00:28:43.535
at the different archives we're
working with, whether those are

00:28:43.535 --> 00:28:46.160
large-scale national
museums, or whether we're

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:49.700
looking at smaller, more local
institutions, which might not

00:28:49.700 --> 00:28:52.790
necessarily have that same
demand as some of the larger,

00:28:52.790 --> 00:28:54.806
national archives.

00:28:54.806 --> 00:28:55.725
How about Wendy?

00:28:59.490 --> 00:29:00.180
Thanks, guys.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:00.780
Interesting.

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:02.010
Really interesting.

00:29:02.010 --> 00:29:03.750
Curious about the
hardware that's

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:06.500
going to be involved,
actually on the site.

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:08.250
And the training that
you're going to have

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:09.900
to give the local folks.

00:29:09.900 --> 00:29:11.897
Sure, want to see it?

00:29:11.897 --> 00:29:13.230
This is our hardware right here.

00:29:13.230 --> 00:29:14.520
I have one next to me.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:17.648
This is the Hovercam
Solo 8 Plus.

00:29:17.648 --> 00:29:18.690
This is what we're using.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:20.200
I was going to use it
as my video camera,

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:22.140
but then I wouldn't be
able to show everyone.

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:24.480
The other is--

00:29:24.480 --> 00:29:25.530
I'm using it right now.

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.540
So I've personally tested
out all the hardware

00:29:27.540 --> 00:29:28.582
that we're going to need.

00:29:28.582 --> 00:29:32.340
It requires a pretty beefy
laptop for a workstation,

00:29:32.340 --> 00:29:35.670
because you're encoding it and
then you're distributing it.

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:38.370
So the hardware--

00:29:38.370 --> 00:29:40.500
My question is like, the
place in Columbus, Ohio

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:44.380
is going to need to get
one of these pieces?

00:29:44.380 --> 00:29:45.210
So, yes.

00:29:45.210 --> 00:29:47.620
We will be working
directly with contractors.

00:29:47.620 --> 00:29:50.820
So issuing workstations
to the assistants

00:29:50.820 --> 00:29:52.890
who are going to be doing this.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:56.670
Another option which
we might do depending

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.800
on how frequently
it's used is assigning

00:29:58.800 --> 00:30:02.680
a workstation per archive
is another way to do it.

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:06.217
We're still investigating
that on what's most efficient.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:10.470
All right.

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:13.480
And that is all the time we have
for this round of questions.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:16.590
Thank you so much, ArcLive.

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:20.130
Now I would like to welcome
to the virtual stage,

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:22.740
Carolyn and Natasha
of P(art)ner.

00:30:25.320 --> 00:30:27.295
Thank you all so much
for joining us today.

00:30:27.295 --> 00:30:28.170
We're Team P(art)ner.

00:30:28.170 --> 00:30:29.360
I'm Carolyn.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:31.230
I'm Natasha.

00:30:31.230 --> 00:30:33.660
We have all been
moved by good art.

00:30:33.660 --> 00:30:36.480
But what happens
when things go wrong?

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:39.510
The city of Seoul
spent $1.4 million

00:30:39.510 --> 00:30:41.790
commissioning renowned
artist Frank Stella

00:30:41.790 --> 00:30:43.560
for a public sculpture
and the community

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:45.390
was so offended by it.

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.090
The city had to plant a
ring of trees around it.

00:30:48.090 --> 00:30:49.590
Unbelievable, right?

00:30:49.590 --> 00:30:52.320
This goes to show that we don't
always connect with the art

00:30:52.320 --> 00:30:56.280
that we see and that large-scale
art is hard to create.

00:30:56.280 --> 00:30:58.530
We're not getting what we
want out of the $2 billion

00:30:58.530 --> 00:31:00.240
that we spend on
public art each year,

00:31:00.240 --> 00:31:04.020
given white men constitute a
majority of local art rosters

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:05.280
and galleries.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:07.380
These trends reveal
that art commissioning

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:09.690
as we know it
prevents art seekers

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:11.640
from finding art creators.

00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:14.640
As a consequence,
artwork in public spaces

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:18.360
doesn't represent local
neighborhoods in our cities.

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:20.660
My experience as a real
estate design manager

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:23.010
speaks to this consequence.

00:31:23.010 --> 00:31:25.190
In looking to create memorable
and local experiences

00:31:25.190 --> 00:31:27.710
for our customers,
I found the process

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:30.170
to be time-consuming to
manage, as items were often

00:31:30.170 --> 00:31:34.190
lost in translation between the
development and artist world.

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:36.440
Most critically, this
extensive process

00:31:36.440 --> 00:31:39.470
did not always result
in rewarding art.

00:31:39.470 --> 00:31:41.840
What I thought were my
singular frustrations

00:31:41.840 --> 00:31:43.970
were actually echoed
throughout the industry

00:31:43.970 --> 00:31:47.230
as we spoke further
with both sides.

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:49.690
It's inspiring to us that
the importance of art

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:51.240
is affirmed by city governments.

00:31:51.240 --> 00:31:55.750
360 of which mandate developers
spend 1% of construction

00:31:55.750 --> 00:31:57.550
costs to provide public art.

00:31:57.550 --> 00:32:00.220
P(art)ner sees this as an
opportunity for local talent

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:03.700
to represent a neighborhood
and increase both real estate

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:06.290
and social value.

00:32:06.290 --> 00:32:08.710
But the benefits
don't just end here.

00:32:08.710 --> 00:32:11.140
Research shows that good
art can create destinations

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:14.140
like Naoshima Island,
increase property values,

00:32:14.140 --> 00:32:17.470
or even stimulate the
broader municipal economy.

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:21.010
P(art)ner aims to harness
the power of art, culture,

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:24.220
and performance to enhance
the urban experience while

00:32:24.220 --> 00:32:26.680
increasing real estate value.

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:28.900
We celebrate local
creative talent

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:32.500
and we do this by helping art
seekers discover, connect with,

00:32:32.500 --> 00:32:34.600
and commission the
most promising talent

00:32:34.600 --> 00:32:37.650
to move their projects forward.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:40.350
P(art)ner comes in with a
solution that connects buyers

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:43.980
looking for local large-scale
art, and sellers who create it,

00:32:43.980 --> 00:32:47.790
as a re-imagined 21st century
public art gallery that

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:50.250
democratizes art commissioning.

00:32:50.250 --> 00:32:52.580
Buyers of art looking to
distinguish their projects

00:32:52.580 --> 00:32:56.880
can virtually discover local,
pre-vetted, high-quality and

00:32:56.880 --> 00:33:00.270
community-oriented creators
of art who know and understand

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:03.150
the neighborhoods they
live and create in.

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:05.970
Buyers and sellers
engage in three features.

00:33:05.970 --> 00:33:09.030
Discover showcases project
proposals and portfolios,

00:33:09.030 --> 00:33:11.640
offering more opportunities
for seekers and creators

00:33:11.640 --> 00:33:12.940
to find each other.

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:14.780
The Connect function
matches portfolios

00:33:14.780 --> 00:33:16.320
with proposals and
goals, providing

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:19.450
collaborative communication
of project vision.

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:22.830
The Commission feature gives
fair pricing and clear steps

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:26.295
to manage contracts
and deliverables.

00:33:26.295 --> 00:33:28.590
We envision a world
where everyone wins.

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:30.840
Art seekers have
direct and fast access

00:33:30.840 --> 00:33:33.460
to a broader, vetted
local talent pool.

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:35.520
A democratized art
commissioning process

00:33:35.520 --> 00:33:39.090
allows local artists to
help set properties apart,

00:33:39.090 --> 00:33:40.590
which then improves
the public realm

00:33:40.590 --> 00:33:43.360
and leads to place-making
with a purpose.

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:46.520
We're competing against creative
place-making agencies, art

00:33:46.520 --> 00:33:48.760
consultants and art
commissioning platforms

00:33:48.760 --> 00:33:52.150
that often only manage one
portion of the process.

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:55.000
But our competitive advantage
empowers art creators

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:57.013
to take charge of
their own success

00:33:57.013 --> 00:33:58.930
through features like
accessing a wider vendor

00:33:58.930 --> 00:34:01.960
ecosystem, curated
educational resources,

00:34:01.960 --> 00:34:06.550
and a protected marketplace that
allows both sides to be bold.

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:09.909
Our business model operates
a freemium subscription

00:34:09.909 --> 00:34:11.650
and fee-based pricing.

00:34:11.650 --> 00:34:13.690
Art seekers pay a
flat subscription fee

00:34:13.690 --> 00:34:15.834
to access creative talent
through commissioning

00:34:15.834 --> 00:34:18.250
and a project execution roadmap.

00:34:18.250 --> 00:34:20.370
We hope to grow our
seekers and creators

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:22.300
pool so that we
can be in 10 cities

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:25.489
at the end of the third year.

00:34:25.489 --> 00:34:26.728
So who are we?

00:34:26.728 --> 00:34:29.409
We are deeply invested in
P(art)ner because it marries

00:34:29.409 --> 00:34:31.989
our core personal missions,
bringing together empowering

00:34:31.989 --> 00:34:34.270
emerging artists,
curated place-making,

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:36.850
and responsible real
estate development.

00:34:36.850 --> 00:34:39.400
Given my experience as
an artist, architect,

00:34:39.400 --> 00:34:41.739
and real estate developer,
I know the ins and outs

00:34:41.739 --> 00:34:43.730
of complex art
projects and how tricky

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:46.870
that can be for both
sides to manage.

00:34:46.870 --> 00:34:48.600
I'm a policy wonk
and startup lover,

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.300
well-versed in bringing
together unlikely partnerships,

00:34:51.300 --> 00:34:53.460
but also invested in
closing the racial wealth

00:34:53.460 --> 00:34:55.350
gap for black and
brown communities

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.990
based on experiences working
in economic and workforce

00:34:57.990 --> 00:34:59.100
development.

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:00.510
The competition
funding will help

00:35:00.510 --> 00:35:02.730
us establish our
virtual presence

00:35:02.730 --> 00:35:04.890
and demonstrate
our first project.

00:35:04.890 --> 00:35:06.840
To scale up, we
will first continue

00:35:06.840 --> 00:35:10.020
to compile our online database
of seekers and creators,

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:12.390
then design a fair
commissioning process,

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:15.150
and finally, pilot with a
percent for art program,

00:35:15.150 --> 00:35:16.520
Seeker.

00:35:16.520 --> 00:35:20.450
During this time of great strife
and uncertainty, many of us

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:23.570
are turning to art as an
escape and form of healing.

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:27.020
We at P(art)ner want to play
a major role in providing

00:35:27.020 --> 00:35:29.900
a re-imagined gallery to
help current artists weather

00:35:29.900 --> 00:35:33.410
the storm and ultimately
enliven places through art.

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.220
Thank you so much for your time.

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:35.720
Thank you.

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:40.640
Thank you both so much.

00:35:40.640 --> 00:35:43.100
All right, let's hear
from Eran, please.

00:35:47.490 --> 00:35:50.580
Thanks, this sounds
really interesting.

00:35:50.580 --> 00:35:51.830
I guess a couple of questions.

00:35:51.830 --> 00:35:55.350
One is you talked about
focusing on large-scale art,

00:35:55.350 --> 00:35:59.290
and I was wondering if
this is the main focus

00:35:59.290 --> 00:36:02.950
that you guys have or is
that just the beginning

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:05.467
that you see for your business.

00:36:05.467 --> 00:36:07.300
And then the second
question I have actually

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:09.520
has to do with
what's potentially

00:36:09.520 --> 00:36:12.460
interesting about your
business, is connecting people

00:36:12.460 --> 00:36:14.470
across different locations,
but at the same time

00:36:14.470 --> 00:36:16.160
you're talking about
the need for local art

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:18.327
so that the local artists
understand the local scene

00:36:18.327 --> 00:36:19.840
and that's how it works.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:24.040
But that actually seems to limit
you because then basically what

00:36:24.040 --> 00:36:27.015
you're saying is, for
this to work well,

00:36:27.015 --> 00:36:29.140
you need local artists,
but then the network effect

00:36:29.140 --> 00:36:31.223
is not that interesting
anymore because we're just

00:36:31.223 --> 00:36:34.478
talking about local
artists and in localities.

00:36:34.478 --> 00:36:37.020
So I was wondering if you could
address both of those points.

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:41.750
Sure, I can take that.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:43.660
So in terms of
large-scale art, I

00:36:43.660 --> 00:36:47.350
think we were very
deliberate in addressing this

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:50.620
because we really want to
be careful about not being

00:36:50.620 --> 00:36:52.930
associated with smaller-scale
art, which I think

00:36:52.930 --> 00:36:56.800
has a lot more competition
of what's already

00:36:56.800 --> 00:36:58.510
being placed in the market.

00:36:58.510 --> 00:37:00.220
And then also
recognizing that one

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:04.360
of the biggest issues in art
commissioning is funding.

00:37:04.360 --> 00:37:07.660
And so it was a very
interesting journey for us

00:37:07.660 --> 00:37:10.330
to realize that on one
hand, artists are always

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:12.208
looking for funding,
but on the other hand,

00:37:12.208 --> 00:37:13.750
in the real estate
development world,

00:37:13.750 --> 00:37:17.260
there is all this funding
that's been bookmarked.

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:20.650
And so the project range can
go from $50,000, on average,

00:37:20.650 --> 00:37:23.080
to $2 million that's
being mandated

00:37:23.080 --> 00:37:26.140
to be spent per project.

00:37:26.140 --> 00:37:28.000
And then in terms
of the local, I

00:37:28.000 --> 00:37:36.370
think that it's recognizing
that for example, the 500

00:37:36.370 --> 00:37:39.580
artists that are
given for public art,

00:37:39.580 --> 00:37:41.710
they do tend to
be national scale.

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:45.640
So we just want to
recognize that we

00:37:45.640 --> 00:37:49.490
want to be more mindful
about the artist process

00:37:49.490 --> 00:37:51.520
in deliberating
and communicating

00:37:51.520 --> 00:37:54.040
with the community so
that the art project that

00:37:54.040 --> 00:37:57.465
is being created is more
reflective of the neighborhood.

00:37:57.465 --> 00:37:58.840
But then also
addressing the fact

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:02.170
that there is a slight bit of
diversity issue in that pool.

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:06.360
Wonderful.

00:38:06.360 --> 00:38:09.650
Next, let's hear from Gui.

00:38:09.650 --> 00:38:11.190
Thank you for the presentation.

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:13.910
My question it's a
little similar to Eran's.

00:38:13.910 --> 00:38:18.260
I just want to develop
that about a local artist.

00:38:18.260 --> 00:38:20.480
I think it's a great idea
to connect local artists

00:38:20.480 --> 00:38:23.060
to public art and do that more.

00:38:23.060 --> 00:38:24.830
There's a whole issue
of gentrification

00:38:24.830 --> 00:38:26.540
in the culture of communities.

00:38:26.540 --> 00:38:28.020
I live in Brooklyn,
for instance,

00:38:28.020 --> 00:38:30.770
and this is a
constant discussion

00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:33.598
about how to represent the
communities that were already

00:38:33.598 --> 00:38:36.140
lived-in, so the neighborhoods
are being replaced, et cetera.

00:38:40.383 --> 00:38:42.800
Something that's good for the
community not necessarily is

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:44.217
going to be good
for real estate--

00:38:46.460 --> 00:38:50.540
make something more expensive
and desirable as an investment.

00:38:50.540 --> 00:38:53.270
Probably if you put one of
your examples, Yayoi Kusama's

00:38:53.270 --> 00:38:55.270
big pumpkin in front of
a building in Brooklyn,

00:38:55.270 --> 00:38:57.880
that's going to sell
for a higher price

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:00.432
than if you get a local
artists, which could eventually

00:39:00.432 --> 00:39:01.640
be good for the neighborhood.

00:39:01.640 --> 00:39:05.350
Say how you see that issue.

00:39:07.950 --> 00:39:08.770
Yeah, absolutely.

00:39:08.770 --> 00:39:10.470
Just jumping in here.

00:39:10.470 --> 00:39:13.170
That's a question that we've
been thinking a lot about, too.

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:16.800
I think ideally on our platform
as we're bringing together

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.110
local creators,
or they don't have

00:39:19.110 --> 00:39:23.370
to be necessarily local
or emerging, but as long

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:26.070
as we're widening
that pool that is

00:39:26.070 --> 00:39:29.700
accessible to large-scale
art seekers, that's really

00:39:29.700 --> 00:39:31.380
what we're in the business for.

00:39:31.380 --> 00:39:35.148
But also specifically, one of
the features that we're still

00:39:35.148 --> 00:39:36.690
flushing out is
thinking through, OK,

00:39:36.690 --> 00:39:39.840
so if you have a large-scale
project that you're putting up

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:43.410
as a proposal, hopefully you
can engage several artists.

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:46.470
Right, and so there might be
five-- some local, some not--

00:39:46.470 --> 00:39:50.580
who are also sharing about their
community and social practice,

00:39:50.580 --> 00:39:52.620
and that might actually
be a really great way

00:39:52.620 --> 00:39:55.530
to engage the
community through art

00:39:55.530 --> 00:40:00.540
that helps with the developer's
approval process in that.

00:40:00.540 --> 00:40:06.090
So we're really hoping to open
up an opportunity for seekers

00:40:06.090 --> 00:40:08.970
to really engage,
and meet and share

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:12.640
their visions with several
artists through the process.

00:40:12.640 --> 00:40:17.940
All right, let's hear
from Phillip please.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:19.070
Hi.

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:24.140
The piece that really--

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:27.400
I think is the most hard
for me to understand

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:30.470
is, you even mentioned you
have a two-sided marketplace,

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:33.810
which, you know, how do
you build credibility?

00:40:33.810 --> 00:40:34.310
Right?

00:40:34.310 --> 00:40:37.830
How do you find a way for
both artists to think,

00:40:37.830 --> 00:40:40.160
like, I can get my stuff
here and how do you

00:40:40.160 --> 00:40:41.660
get developers who
are used to going

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:43.400
to a certain set of
art buyers that they

00:40:43.400 --> 00:40:45.860
trust to go to here instead?

00:40:45.860 --> 00:40:47.570
I think that that's
the biggest question,

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:50.030
and I didn't see anything
in the proposal that

00:40:50.030 --> 00:40:52.640
really addresses that head on.

00:40:52.640 --> 00:40:55.912
This is our last answer.

00:40:55.912 --> 00:40:58.720
Carolyn, do you want
to take that one?

00:40:58.720 --> 00:41:02.100
Yeah, so we are starting
off with our strategy

00:41:02.100 --> 00:41:04.350
working with non-profit
organizations,

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:07.260
and also working with
local developers.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:12.360
So we're really excited to
pilot with Sebastian Mariscal

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:13.080
in Somerville.

00:41:13.080 --> 00:41:15.450
So he's launching a
development called

00:41:15.450 --> 00:41:18.450
CALA, which is really centered
around community and art

00:41:18.450 --> 00:41:22.980
and he has over 400
square feet of mural space

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:24.010
for artists to fill.

00:41:24.010 --> 00:41:28.350
And so we want to be partnering
with mission-driven developers

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:31.080
to start off with, and
we also have connections

00:41:31.080 --> 00:41:33.630
with great non-profit
organizations

00:41:33.630 --> 00:41:35.340
like the Dorchester Art Project.

00:41:35.340 --> 00:41:37.890
And so they have
artists in residence

00:41:37.890 --> 00:41:41.140
that we would love to work with
and feature in our platform.

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:43.750
So that's our scale up strategy.

00:41:43.750 --> 00:41:47.250
Yeah, and then speaking to
the developer point of view,

00:41:47.250 --> 00:41:51.660
I think that the current
practices right now

00:41:51.660 --> 00:41:53.730
is basically a
developer will either

00:41:53.730 --> 00:41:56.400
choose to spend a lot of
money on an art consultant,

00:41:56.400 --> 00:42:00.630
or if their budget is
about $100,000 or less,

00:42:00.630 --> 00:42:03.450
then they will take
it upon themselves

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:06.900
to find the artist which
means that they're not really

00:42:06.900 --> 00:42:08.940
optimizing this opportunity.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:11.370
And the most interesting
thing that we've

00:42:11.370 --> 00:42:13.800
heard in all of our
research interviews

00:42:13.800 --> 00:42:17.100
is the fact that even when
developers are spending up

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:20.160
to $500,000 in just
consulting fees,

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:22.770
they've always mentioned
that at the very end

00:42:22.770 --> 00:42:26.040
they were unsatisfied with the
amount of art that was catered

00:42:26.040 --> 00:42:28.360
to the local community.

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:31.020
Because I think that
there is a huge branding

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:33.390
opportunity that's being
missed by developers

00:42:33.390 --> 00:42:36.840
because a lot of them do want to
capture and show that they have

00:42:36.840 --> 00:42:40.320
good faith towards the community
and if you're engaging artists

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:42.660
on a national scale,
you're not really

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.400
optimizing for the
community buy-in.

00:42:45.400 --> 00:42:47.670
So I think there's
definitely a mix that

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:51.630
needs to be had in how
you structure your art

00:42:51.630 --> 00:42:53.130
and where you want
to be placing it,

00:42:53.130 --> 00:42:58.450
but there definitely is a lack
on the local talent right now.

00:42:58.450 --> 00:42:58.950
All right.

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:01.290
Thank you so much, P(art)ner.

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:06.300
Next up, I would like
to welcome Elements.

00:43:06.300 --> 00:43:08.145
Hi everyone, I'm Maria.

00:43:08.145 --> 00:43:09.420
I'm Michael.

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:11.340
And we are Team ELEMENTS.

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:15.640
ELEMENTS is design
learning in a box.

00:43:15.640 --> 00:43:18.850
ELEMENTS unlocks the full
potential of every student.

00:43:18.850 --> 00:43:23.590
Our kits teach creative thinking
to students ages 10 to 14,

00:43:23.590 --> 00:43:26.260
preparing them for the
emerging jobs and challenges

00:43:26.260 --> 00:43:29.620
of the 21st century and
developing their human skills

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:32.550
in the age of automation.

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:34.320
Parents and teachers
increasingly

00:43:34.320 --> 00:43:36.960
acknowledge the value
of design education.

00:43:36.960 --> 00:43:38.550
In the short term,
they see students

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:40.800
build fundamental
design skills and engage

00:43:40.800 --> 00:43:42.390
in project-based learning.

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:45.623
Schools see higher graduation
rates and improved test scores.

00:43:45.623 --> 00:43:47.040
In the long term,
whether students

00:43:47.040 --> 00:43:49.140
become designers
or anything else,

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.570
they build creative
problem solving skills,

00:43:51.570 --> 00:43:53.970
they build a lifelong
appreciation of design,

00:43:53.970 --> 00:43:57.300
and they hone their spatial
and iterative thinking skills.

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:00.120
Despite all of that,
arts education in the US

00:44:00.120 --> 00:44:01.560
has been on a steady decline.

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:05.130
As of 2008, only about a
quarter of graduating seniors

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:09.930
reported having any access
to a visual arts education.

00:44:09.930 --> 00:44:12.750
And outside of public
school systems,

00:44:12.750 --> 00:44:16.500
access to design education
is expensive and exclusive.

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:19.680
Current options like
university pre-college programs

00:44:19.680 --> 00:44:24.450
or a specialized creative arts
school can cost about $2,000

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.370
for only a two-week course.

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:29.800
That's why we've come
up with ELEMENTS.

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:32.230
Our kits teach different
elements of design,

00:44:32.230 --> 00:44:35.350
such as deployable
structures, stop motion,

00:44:35.350 --> 00:44:40.040
and pattern design through
open-ended challenges.

00:44:40.040 --> 00:44:42.790
ELEMENTS has a three-part
value proposition.

00:44:42.790 --> 00:44:44.090
Design for All.

00:44:44.090 --> 00:44:47.300
ELEMENTS brings design thinking,
project-based learning,

00:44:47.300 --> 00:44:50.270
and creative problem
solving to future creatives,

00:44:50.270 --> 00:44:53.240
future professionals, and
future everything else.

00:44:53.240 --> 00:44:54.530
Tactile Learning.

00:44:54.530 --> 00:44:56.900
ELEMENTS offers physical
learning materials

00:44:56.900 --> 00:44:59.150
and tactile design
challenges paired

00:44:59.150 --> 00:45:01.370
with an online
platform for students

00:45:01.370 --> 00:45:03.440
to share their creative work.

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:05.360
And finally,
ELEMENTS is designed

00:45:05.360 --> 00:45:08.540
to develop the uniquely human
skills of the 21st century

00:45:08.540 --> 00:45:11.200
professional.

00:45:11.200 --> 00:45:12.400
So, why us?

00:45:12.400 --> 00:45:14.730
We believe that we're uniquely
suited for this venture

00:45:14.730 --> 00:45:16.990
and we are really
motivated about this issue.

00:45:16.990 --> 00:45:19.390
We truly believe that
everyone should have access

00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:20.860
to design education.

00:45:20.860 --> 00:45:23.410
We will implement our own
design teaching knowledge

00:45:23.410 --> 00:45:26.140
and techniques into
the logic of the game.

00:45:26.140 --> 00:45:28.810
Our venture actually
began as a partnership,

00:45:28.810 --> 00:45:30.580
where both Michael
and I were looking

00:45:30.580 --> 00:45:34.300
at experimental teaching methods
and together we ran teaching

00:45:34.300 --> 00:45:36.640
seminars for MIT students.

00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:41.700
That original collaboration
grew into our current venture.

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:44.670
So ELEMENTS has three
cultural touchstones.

00:45:44.670 --> 00:45:46.920
The first, ELEMENTS is generous.

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:49.560
Serious design education
demands an openness

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:52.540
to outside voices
and unorthodox ideas.

00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:54.780
The second, ELEMENTS
is approachable.

00:45:54.780 --> 00:45:57.450
Design is often considered
elite or snobby.

00:45:57.450 --> 00:46:00.120
We try to defy those
stereotypes, both in access

00:46:00.120 --> 00:46:01.560
and our attitude.

00:46:01.560 --> 00:46:03.810
And third, ELEMENTS
is experimental.

00:46:03.810 --> 00:46:05.610
Our commitment to
experimentation

00:46:05.610 --> 00:46:07.830
applies to our teaching
practices, our design

00:46:07.830 --> 00:46:10.770
practices, and our
venture at large.

00:46:10.770 --> 00:46:13.320
So to those ends, we've
assembled a team of mentors,

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:15.770
both within MIT and in
the broader community,

00:46:15.770 --> 00:46:17.820
for helping us grow the
venture and maintain

00:46:17.820 --> 00:46:20.330
those values moving forward.

00:46:20.330 --> 00:46:22.310
So back to the kits and
a few of the prototypes

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:23.330
we're working on.

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:25.910
This is the curriculum for
Color, Material and Pigment

00:46:25.910 --> 00:46:26.810
Formulation.

00:46:26.810 --> 00:46:28.310
With this, a
student would go out

00:46:28.310 --> 00:46:31.400
into their local [INAUDIBLE],,
gather a series of objects,

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:33.710
and then have the
resources and instructions

00:46:33.710 --> 00:46:37.610
to transform those objects into
workable paints and pigments.

00:46:37.610 --> 00:46:40.250
Another prototype here that
looked at rule based drawing,

00:46:40.250 --> 00:46:43.310
we tested in the fall
within MIT MArch students,

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:45.320
and then again over
the winter at MIT

00:46:45.320 --> 00:46:49.880
Splash with junior high and high
school students from the area.

00:46:49.880 --> 00:46:52.160
OK, so now let's have
a look at the market.

00:46:52.160 --> 00:46:56.150
The US toy market averages over
$20 billion dollars per year,

00:46:56.150 --> 00:46:58.790
with building sets,
games, and arts and crafts

00:46:58.790 --> 00:47:01.970
accounting for
25% of that total.

00:47:01.970 --> 00:47:05.480
Our research shows that there is
a trend in educational products

00:47:05.480 --> 00:47:08.360
shifting away from creative
subjects to science

00:47:08.360 --> 00:47:11.360
and engineering subjects by
junior high and high school,

00:47:11.360 --> 00:47:15.240
leaving older students without
access to creative curriculum.

00:47:15.240 --> 00:47:17.120
That's your one minute warning.

00:47:17.120 --> 00:47:20.000
So that's where we see
ELEMENTS fitting in.

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:22.905
We believe we will occupy
this gap in the market,

00:47:22.905 --> 00:47:25.280
and we believe that design
learning and creative thinking

00:47:25.280 --> 00:47:27.290
should be encouraged
at all ages.

00:47:27.290 --> 00:47:30.050
That's why our products
offer creative curriculum

00:47:30.050 --> 00:47:34.710
to an age group where these
subjects are being phased out.

00:47:34.710 --> 00:47:38.400
So ELEMENTS will launch as a
direct-to-consumer online sales

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:40.440
company, and we'll be
targeting middle school

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:42.270
students and their
parents and families

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:43.740
as our beachhead market.

00:47:43.740 --> 00:47:46.200
This is a sweet spot for us
because junior high students

00:47:46.200 --> 00:47:48.390
are still exploring a
variety of subjects,

00:47:48.390 --> 00:47:50.040
their parents are
highly involved,

00:47:50.040 --> 00:47:52.830
and they're not yet fixated
on college admissions.

00:47:52.830 --> 00:47:54.780
In years two and
three of the venture,

00:47:54.780 --> 00:47:57.750
we plan to expand our market
and offer wholesale curriculum

00:47:57.750 --> 00:48:00.536
to public schools
and corporate teams.

00:48:00.536 --> 00:48:03.700
So with the help of MIT's
Creative Arts Competition,

00:48:03.700 --> 00:48:06.640
we'll develop our initial
line of six curriculum kits,

00:48:06.640 --> 00:48:08.410
anticipating a
monthly subscription

00:48:08.410 --> 00:48:10.050
model in the future.

00:48:10.050 --> 00:48:12.250
We'll establish partnerships
with manufacturers

00:48:12.250 --> 00:48:15.040
and begin shifting our
production out-of-house,

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:16.960
and we'll finalize
our at-home prototype

00:48:16.960 --> 00:48:20.260
kits and our online sharing
platform for a soft launch

00:48:20.260 --> 00:48:21.580
this summer.

00:48:21.580 --> 00:48:23.560
And finally, what is our vision?

00:48:23.560 --> 00:48:26.980
Well, we imagine a society which
supports future generations

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:30.430
of entrepreneurs, designers,
makers, and innovators,

00:48:30.430 --> 00:48:32.140
and where access
to design education

00:48:32.140 --> 00:48:34.690
is available to all,
regardless of background

00:48:34.690 --> 00:48:36.850
or economic status.

00:48:36.850 --> 00:48:37.690
Thank you.

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:39.360
Thank you.

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:42.420
All right, thank
you so, so much.

00:48:42.420 --> 00:48:46.950
And let us turn to
our esteemed panel.

00:48:46.950 --> 00:48:49.393
Let's see, Eran.

00:48:49.393 --> 00:48:50.810
Cool, thanks for
the presentation.

00:48:50.810 --> 00:48:54.000
It looks super cool and
probably really fun to use.

00:48:54.000 --> 00:48:57.960
My question is really about
how you plan to get this out

00:48:57.960 --> 00:48:59.160
there and market it.

00:48:59.160 --> 00:49:00.750
This seems like a
consumer play to me.

00:49:00.750 --> 00:49:02.125
You said that
you're not planning

00:49:02.125 --> 00:49:05.100
to target schools initially.

00:49:05.100 --> 00:49:07.560
And so if you're going
directly after parents

00:49:07.560 --> 00:49:09.300
and junior high
school kids, how do

00:49:09.300 --> 00:49:12.210
you compete with the
billions of marketing dollars

00:49:12.210 --> 00:49:15.840
that are already going out there
by all the major companies?

00:49:15.840 --> 00:49:16.410
That's true.

00:49:16.410 --> 00:49:17.730
It's a real challenge for us.

00:49:17.730 --> 00:49:20.010
We have a few strategies
to get started.

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:24.660
The first is to begin locally
and use public institutions

00:49:24.660 --> 00:49:26.640
like libraries as
a place to launch

00:49:26.640 --> 00:49:30.000
these, gain publicity,
one-on-one, in person

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:31.440
with people.

00:49:31.440 --> 00:49:33.900
And then there's
specific user segments

00:49:33.900 --> 00:49:36.720
that we're looking at, like the
home school community, which

00:49:36.720 --> 00:49:38.040
is very, very plugged in.

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:40.420
They have their own
culture in a sense.

00:49:40.420 --> 00:49:43.110
And so we're targeting
those individuals subsets

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:44.610
of customers, and
we're going to try

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:46.920
to market to them individually.

00:49:46.920 --> 00:49:50.160
But I would say the
grassroots, on the ground,

00:49:50.160 --> 00:49:54.990
in libraries and communities
and after-school centers, that's

00:49:54.990 --> 00:49:59.670
how we get the publicity that
we think people will react to.

00:49:59.670 --> 00:50:02.460
All right, Josh.

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:04.020
Nice presentation.

00:50:04.020 --> 00:50:06.990
And of course, it's
an interesting time

00:50:06.990 --> 00:50:08.950
and a good time for
a product like this.

