00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:57.156
Up next, we've got
Material Futures Lab.

00:54:57.156 --> 00:54:58.650
Hi there.

00:54:58.650 --> 00:55:00.740
So if we look around
the Zoom call--

00:55:00.740 --> 00:55:02.340
and now that we're all home--

00:55:02.340 --> 00:55:04.380
as well in our
closets, we can quickly

00:55:04.380 --> 00:55:08.230
determine that we all wear
a lot of colored clothing.

00:55:08.230 --> 00:55:10.710
And that's awesome,
because colored clothing

00:55:10.710 --> 00:55:13.440
makes us feel and look great.

00:55:13.440 --> 00:55:16.800
And actually, the practice
of coloring textiles

00:55:16.800 --> 00:55:19.590
has been around for
thousands of years

00:55:19.590 --> 00:55:22.830
and is a culturally
significant practice.

00:55:22.830 --> 00:55:25.500
The Navajo tribe, for
instance, in the United States,

00:55:25.500 --> 00:55:27.720
creates these beautiful
weaving charts,

00:55:27.720 --> 00:55:31.170
such as the one you see on
screen, to document and keep

00:55:31.170 --> 00:55:34.560
track of the plant matter
that they use to create

00:55:34.560 --> 00:55:36.270
their colored dye stuffs.

00:55:36.270 --> 00:55:38.430
But unfortunately, a
lot of these practices

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:41.390
are only capable to be
done on a small scale

00:55:41.390 --> 00:55:43.260
and they're not easily scalable.

00:55:43.260 --> 00:55:44.940
So we end up looking
at a lot of images

00:55:44.940 --> 00:55:48.930
like this when we think
of the textile industry.

00:55:48.930 --> 00:55:51.320
Unfortunately, the
textile industry

00:55:51.320 --> 00:55:57.640
is actually responsible for
20% of global water pollution

00:55:57.640 --> 00:56:00.610
and it's the second-most
polluting industry

00:56:00.610 --> 00:56:02.450
after the oil industry.

00:56:02.450 --> 00:56:05.050
And so the current method
of coloring fabrics

00:56:05.050 --> 00:56:08.200
involves using synthetic
dyes and these are often

00:56:08.200 --> 00:56:11.320
treated with toxic
chemicals and heavy metals.

00:56:11.320 --> 00:56:13.210
They do not
decompose, and so that

00:56:13.210 --> 00:56:16.330
means the same dye stuffs that
were used to color fabrics

00:56:16.330 --> 00:56:21.070
150 years ago are still
persisting in our soils

00:56:21.070 --> 00:56:22.660
and in our waterways.

00:56:22.660 --> 00:56:24.820
And unfortunately, their
plant dye counterparts

00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:26.320
are actually not much better.

00:56:26.320 --> 00:56:29.440
Plants are incredibly
resource-intensive,

00:56:29.440 --> 00:56:32.980
they require acres of land,
years of growing time,

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:35.830
and several hundred
liters of water to grow.

00:56:35.830 --> 00:56:38.530
And in fact, indigo farmers
in the United States

00:56:38.530 --> 00:56:41.230
have reported that it
requires them to grow up

00:56:41.230 --> 00:56:45.160
to 13 acres of indigo
dye plants to color

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:48.300
just one acre of cotton fiber.

00:56:48.300 --> 00:56:52.030
And so to put the global
problem into perspective,

00:56:52.030 --> 00:56:55.480
the total market size of dyes
and pigments manufacturing

00:56:55.480 --> 00:57:01.090
is estimated at $49 billion
and is growing at 5% per year

00:57:01.090 --> 00:57:03.370
due to sustainable innovation.

00:57:03.370 --> 00:57:06.430
We see the textile
industry, specifically,

00:57:06.430 --> 00:57:08.470
starting to respond
to these changes,

00:57:08.470 --> 00:57:12.160
to promote more sustainable
products and processes

00:57:12.160 --> 00:57:15.370
due to increasing regulations
on wastewater treatment,

00:57:15.370 --> 00:57:17.650
and consumer preferences
for environmentally

00:57:17.650 --> 00:57:21.100
friendly processes.

00:57:21.100 --> 00:57:23.710
At Material Futures
Lab, we have developed

00:57:23.710 --> 00:57:26.020
a process which
turns microorganisms

00:57:26.020 --> 00:57:28.240
into miniature dye factories.

00:57:28.240 --> 00:57:31.120
We are able to produce really
brightly-colored pigments,

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:35.110
such as the purple-blue one you
see on screen, that don't fade

00:57:35.110 --> 00:57:37.390
due to light or in washing.

00:57:37.390 --> 00:57:40.230
We have proven our process
at the bench scale,

00:57:40.230 --> 00:57:44.400
and we are now beginning to
scale our production process.

00:57:44.400 --> 00:57:47.850
Some of the key benefits of this
approach to producing pigments

00:57:47.850 --> 00:57:50.760
is that our powders can
be easily substituted

00:57:50.760 --> 00:57:53.340
for synthetic pigments
that are used currently.

00:57:53.340 --> 00:57:55.230
We can control our
growing conditions

00:57:55.230 --> 00:57:58.330
to ensure high yields
and consistent color.

00:57:58.330 --> 00:58:01.410
And we can scale our process
using existing manufacturing

00:58:01.410 --> 00:58:03.120
to meet global demand.

00:58:03.120 --> 00:58:06.420
We aim to restore the
tradition of natural dyeing

00:58:06.420 --> 00:58:10.470
on a large scale by leveraging
biotechnology and process

00:58:10.470 --> 00:58:11.760
engineering.

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:14.310
We see our technology
actually translating

00:58:14.310 --> 00:58:16.140
across multiple scales.

00:58:16.140 --> 00:58:17.880
Despite the work
that we're doing

00:58:17.880 --> 00:58:20.610
being mainly at the
scale of a cell,

00:58:20.610 --> 00:58:22.560
this type of
sustainable production

00:58:22.560 --> 00:58:25.230
actually results in cleaner
and safer conditions

00:58:25.230 --> 00:58:26.520
of the dye house.

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:29.670
As well, we promote a
more sustainable economy

00:58:29.670 --> 00:58:31.530
and global market.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:33.700
So we are really
excited, actually,

00:58:33.700 --> 00:58:38.130
to be announcing our partnership
with lululemon athletica.

00:58:38.130 --> 00:58:40.470
We've been in contact with
them for a while, and even

00:58:40.470 --> 00:58:42.432
through the
coronavirus era, we're

00:58:42.432 --> 00:58:43.890
going to be applying
our technology

00:58:43.890 --> 00:58:47.790
to color their athletic
wear and their leggings.

00:58:47.790 --> 00:58:49.500
They're an incredible
partner, they're

00:58:49.500 --> 00:58:51.690
leaders for sustainable
fashion, and they're

00:58:51.690 --> 00:58:53.610
reshaping the fashion
industry to adopt

00:58:53.610 --> 00:58:56.970
more sustainable technology,
and we're privileged and humbled

00:58:56.970 --> 00:58:59.280
to have their support.

00:58:59.280 --> 00:59:02.250
Joe and myself bring
a wealth of experience

00:59:02.250 --> 00:59:04.650
in couture fashion,
sustainable design,

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:07.740
including textile design
and metabolic engineering.

00:59:07.740 --> 00:59:10.590
We're also supported by amazing
mentors, such as Dr. Valerie

00:59:10.590 --> 00:59:11.310
Ward.

00:59:11.310 --> 00:59:13.780
She is by training a
bioprocess chemical engineer--

00:59:13.780 --> 00:59:14.530
[DING] One minute.

00:59:14.530 --> 00:59:17.640
And is supporting
our scale-up process.

00:59:17.640 --> 00:59:19.470
Aithan is also an
artist by training,

00:59:19.470 --> 00:59:21.580
and involved with the
Sloan School at MIT.

00:59:21.580 --> 00:59:23.370
He has been providing
us with mentorship

00:59:23.370 --> 00:59:26.280
on how to build out our art
space technology actually

00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:28.990
into a sustainable
business model.

00:59:28.990 --> 00:59:35.220
So we plan to use the $15,000
from the MIT Creative Arts

00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:38.340
Competition actually to
file a patent on our process

00:59:38.340 --> 00:59:41.580
and to start scaling our
manufacturing to meet demand.

00:59:41.580 --> 00:59:43.770
So at Material Futures
Lab, we believe

00:59:43.770 --> 00:59:46.740
in a world that's designed
with and for nature,

00:59:46.740 --> 00:59:49.200
and we hope to bring this
technology to your closets

00:59:49.200 --> 00:59:51.000
and Zoom calls very soon.

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:53.775
Thanks so much, and we will
now take your questions

00:59:53.775 --> 00:59:54.525
and your comments.

00:59:54.525 --> 00:59:56.110
Thanks.

00:59:56.110 --> 00:59:56.610
All right.

