like paint manufacturers,

01:02:45.710 --> 01:02:47.652
artist paints,
plastics manufacturers,

01:02:47.652 --> 01:02:49.860
and we're in contact with
a bunch of those companies,

01:02:49.860 --> 01:02:51.860
as well.

01:02:51.860 --> 01:02:53.210
Wonderful.

01:02:53.210 --> 01:02:54.680
Wendy.

01:02:54.680 --> 01:02:57.230
Oh.

01:02:57.230 --> 01:02:59.170
You kind of addressed
it all, but I was just

01:02:59.170 --> 01:03:01.170
thinking about what are
the other uses for this,

01:03:01.170 --> 01:03:03.920
and like, why did you pick the
clothing industry, as opposed

01:03:03.920 --> 01:03:06.570
to something it might be a
little easier to get into?

01:03:06.570 --> 01:03:07.690
So--

01:03:07.690 --> 01:03:08.360
Yeah, no.

01:03:08.360 --> 01:03:09.860
It's a very fair point.

01:03:09.860 --> 01:03:13.100
We see that paints and
plastics, they definitely

01:03:13.100 --> 01:03:14.400
have a use for these.

01:03:14.400 --> 01:03:16.890
They're less consumer-facing
in their manufacturing,

01:03:16.890 --> 01:03:19.140
and so we think
that environmentally

01:03:19.140 --> 01:03:22.220
and sustainably we want to
be promoting that message.

01:03:22.220 --> 01:03:26.330
And so, textiles is a
really great way to do that.

01:03:26.330 --> 01:03:27.170
We all wear clothes.

01:03:27.170 --> 01:03:27.670
Well--

01:03:27.670 --> 01:03:27.845
Yeah.

01:03:27.845 --> 01:03:28.345
[CHUCKLING]

01:03:28.345 --> 01:03:29.200
All right.

01:03:29.200 --> 01:03:33.420
And I think I have time
for one more from Eran.

01:03:33.420 --> 01:03:33.920
Cool.

01:03:33.920 --> 01:03:36.920
So, can you actually
make all the colors?

01:03:36.920 --> 01:03:40.910
Yeah, so we're producing a
blue-purple pigment right now.

01:03:40.910 --> 01:03:42.380
We're also looking at red.

01:03:42.380 --> 01:03:46.490
The idea is to go trichromatic
so that we can mix.

01:03:46.490 --> 01:03:48.240
OK, but-- so that's
not a concern, right?

01:03:48.240 --> 01:03:50.180
Like I imagine
that the buyers are

01:03:50.180 --> 01:03:52.290
going to want to have a
large palette of colors,

01:03:52.290 --> 01:03:54.248
you know, that's probably
what they're used to.

01:03:54.248 --> 01:03:58.280
And so that's not like a
technical challenge for you?

01:03:58.280 --> 01:04:01.460
It's something that we believe
we can really overcome.

01:04:01.460 --> 01:04:04.080
As well, in our
conversations we hear a lot

01:04:04.080 --> 01:04:07.060
that blue and red pigments
tend to be the most toxic.

01:04:07.060 --> 01:04:09.560
And so those are actually the
ones, if we can produce those,

01:04:09.560 --> 01:04:11.360
they provide a really
good alternative.

01:04:11.360 --> 01:04:14.900
There's a number of yellow
and kind of orange pigments,

01:04:14.900 --> 01:04:17.720
like turmeric and things like
that, which are manufactured

01:04:17.720 --> 01:04:18.860
for food processing.

01:04:18.860 --> 01:04:21.410
So being able to produce
blue pigments, for instance,

01:04:21.410 --> 01:04:26.140
that are stable to heat
and light has a value.

01:04:26.140 --> 01:04:28.660
Cool.

01:04:28.660 --> 01:04:29.535
All right, wonderful.

01:04:29.535 --> 01:04:30.035
Thank you.

01:04:30.035 --> 01:04:31.590
Thank you all for that.

01:04:31.590 --> 01:04:33.960
I think at this point,
we will now move on.

01:04:33.960 --> 01:04:37.625
Last but not least,
to Team UnMake.

01:04:37.625 --> 01:04:39.850
[INAUDIBLE] sustainable
and natural materials

01:04:39.850 --> 01:04:42.360
in the art production process.

01:04:42.360 --> 01:04:44.730
The idea originated
10 years ago when

01:04:44.730 --> 01:04:47.750
I started experimenting
with sustainable materials

01:04:47.750 --> 01:04:50.180
while working with some of
the most influential artists

01:04:50.180 --> 01:04:51.540
in the world.

01:04:51.540 --> 01:04:54.390
Their practices made a huge
impact on my thought process,

01:04:54.390 --> 01:04:57.290
and I try to convert
it into this company.

01:04:57.290 --> 01:05:00.640
Sustainability plus art
is a multicultural concept

01:05:00.640 --> 01:05:04.660
that is being followed across
several cultures in the world.

01:05:04.660 --> 01:05:07.710
And millennials are leading
this race, currently.

01:05:07.710 --> 01:05:11.080
Our primary research
proves that tens of artists

01:05:11.080 --> 01:05:13.530
want to really work with
sustainable materials

01:05:13.530 --> 01:05:17.530
and are highly inclined
towards sustainability.

01:05:17.530 --> 01:05:22.420
The online art sales of
a market of $4.6 billion

01:05:22.420 --> 01:05:26.150
and this sustainable product
sales of $150 billion

01:05:26.150 --> 01:05:28.960
just goes on to prove
that sustainability

01:05:28.960 --> 01:05:30.850
is on a rapid rise.

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:33.550
So the whole world is moving
towards sustainability,

01:05:33.550 --> 01:05:35.680
the online art
markets are rising,

01:05:35.680 --> 01:05:38.450
and the consumer section
is growing rapidly.

01:05:38.450 --> 01:05:41.890
But this market is
currently very segmented

01:05:41.890 --> 01:05:46.600
and it needs a huge
reconnection and a bridge.

01:05:46.600 --> 01:05:48.516
And that's where we come in.

01:05:48.516 --> 01:05:51.920
We lie at the intersection
of the sustainability market,

01:05:51.920 --> 01:05:56.160
the art industry, and the
rising consumer section.

01:05:56.160 --> 01:05:58.970
We are bridging the gap between
sustainability and the art

01:05:58.970 --> 01:06:02.000
industry by connecting
sustainable, locally-sourced

01:06:02.000 --> 01:06:06.150
materials, and those
materials producers.

01:06:06.150 --> 01:06:08.180
This is our production strategy.

01:06:08.180 --> 01:06:12.580
An artist uploads their
work on our platform.

01:06:12.580 --> 01:06:16.730
We produce it using recycled
paper and recycled ink.

01:06:16.730 --> 01:06:20.480
Then, we use the city-specific
metal, or bamboo,

01:06:20.480 --> 01:06:23.060
or wooden frame to reproduce it.

01:06:23.060 --> 01:06:26.020
And then, we distribute
it to the customer.

01:06:26.020 --> 01:06:28.250
Our scaling strategy is simple.

01:06:28.250 --> 01:06:31.270
We are only going to scale up
city wise, based on the art

01:06:31.270 --> 01:06:33.610
awareness, ease of
production and delivery,

01:06:33.610 --> 01:06:37.050
and sustainability
consciousness of the cities.

01:06:37.050 --> 01:06:39.840
And we are going to deliver
only within the city premises

01:06:39.840 --> 01:06:44.440
to avoid shipping and keeping
the carbon footprint minimal.

01:06:44.440 --> 01:06:46.130
We have started
operations in two art

01:06:46.130 --> 01:06:48.960
conscious global cities,
New York and Bombay,

01:06:48.960 --> 01:06:53.100
and our research proves that
they are totally logistically

01:06:53.100 --> 01:06:55.870
suitable for our work.

01:06:55.870 --> 01:06:57.810
Our value proposition
is multi-fold.

01:06:57.810 --> 01:07:00.513
We are encouraging a
global community of artists

01:07:00.513 --> 01:07:01.930
across the world
to come together.

01:07:01.930 --> 01:07:04.870
We are fostering a
combination of local

01:07:04.870 --> 01:07:07.260
plus global
intercultural fusion.

01:07:07.260 --> 01:07:09.785
We are cultivating
environmental consciousness.

01:07:09.785 --> 01:07:11.410
We are providing
business opportunities

01:07:11.410 --> 01:07:14.040
to emerging and individual
artists across the world.

01:07:14.040 --> 01:07:16.930
And we are enhancing
the local art and craft

01:07:16.930 --> 01:07:20.430
and boosting the local
economy of the cities.

01:07:20.430 --> 01:07:23.410
We are going to partner with
artists, material suppliers,

01:07:23.410 --> 01:07:26.020
local craftspeople,
delivery vendors,

01:07:26.020 --> 01:07:27.790
as well as the local
printing studios who

01:07:27.790 --> 01:07:29.470
will give us the
space and let us

01:07:29.470 --> 01:07:31.940
do the work in their studios.

01:07:31.940 --> 01:07:34.120
Our revenue model comprises
of the royalties held

01:07:34.120 --> 01:07:36.420
by the artists and
our production cost,

01:07:36.420 --> 01:07:40.450
and we are going to charge
15% of the total cost.

01:07:40.450 --> 01:07:42.880
This is a hypothesis
customer scenario

01:07:42.880 --> 01:07:46.430
in which a Japanese artist
puts her work on our platform.

01:07:46.430 --> 01:07:51.320
A Bombay girl likes the work
and orders it on UnMake.

01:07:51.320 --> 01:07:54.330
Our in-house craftsman Ravi
prints the Japanese art

01:07:54.330 --> 01:07:57.655
on old Indian Dhoti cloth
and with natural pigment

01:07:57.655 --> 01:07:59.440
and ancient print technique.

01:07:59.440 --> 01:08:01.780
And then the artwork is
delivered to Madhuri.

01:08:01.780 --> 01:08:05.050
She loves the artwork framed
with vernacular Indian material

01:08:05.050 --> 01:08:07.400
and the Japanese art.

01:08:07.400 --> 01:08:10.600
We have already just received
750-plus registrations

01:08:10.600 --> 01:08:13.750
from all over the world, and
50-plus high-quality artworks

01:08:13.750 --> 01:08:15.320
are there on our
website already,

01:08:15.320 --> 01:08:17.410
which you can check
on our website.

01:08:17.410 --> 01:08:19.720
We're being supported by
MIT Sandbox Innovation

01:08:19.720 --> 01:08:21.810
Fund and the MIT
Innovation Initiative,

01:08:21.810 --> 01:08:25.080
and of course, MIT
Creative Arts Competition.

01:08:25.080 --> 01:08:26.790
The images that you
see in the background

01:08:26.790 --> 01:08:31.160
are all signed by the artists
from around the world.

01:08:31.160 --> 01:08:34.930
With the $15K, we are going
to test the physical product,

01:08:34.930 --> 01:08:38.300
and this money will also
help us in the outreach.

01:08:38.300 --> 01:08:42.270
We're researching about
the competitive analysis,

01:08:42.270 --> 01:08:45.899
and we figured that the business
model of the current players

01:08:45.899 --> 01:08:48.200
does not deal with
sustainability at all.

01:08:48.200 --> 01:08:50.340
And we lie somewhere
between players

01:08:50.340 --> 01:08:53.189
like Etsy and Framebridge,
and we provide a sustainable

01:08:53.189 --> 01:08:56.100
alternative to them.

01:08:56.100 --> 01:08:58.569
Our team comprises of
professional artists,

01:08:58.569 --> 01:09:00.970
financial experts,
technical experts,

01:09:00.970 --> 01:09:03.490
and we've all been working
together for several years.

01:09:03.490 --> 01:09:06.279
I am an architecture
student here at MIT,

01:09:06.279 --> 01:09:08.859
and I recently headed
the MIT India Initiative,

01:09:08.859 --> 01:09:10.569
which was MIT's
largest student-run

01:09:10.569 --> 01:09:13.310
initiative in India.

01:09:13.310 --> 01:09:16.370
Our team also comprises of
professors and advisors who

01:09:16.370 --> 01:09:19.029
have been working in the art
sector for several years,

01:09:19.029 --> 01:09:21.970
in addition to the domain and
technical expertise provided

01:09:21.970 --> 01:09:25.682
by the MIT Sandbox
and Arts at MIT.

01:09:25.682 --> 01:09:27.140
We've been partnering
with hundreds

01:09:27.140 --> 01:09:29.720
of several local artists
in India and the US,

01:09:29.720 --> 01:09:32.540
and they're very happy
to team up with us.

01:09:32.540 --> 01:09:35.390
We're also teaming up with
prospective influencers

01:09:35.390 --> 01:09:37.490
like Stella McCartney,
Bobby Sager,

01:09:37.490 --> 01:09:42.090
and these people are very happy
to be involved in our project.

01:09:42.090 --> 01:09:44.220
We have conducted
several workshops

01:09:44.220 --> 01:09:45.750
related to local material use.

01:09:45.750 --> 01:09:47.939
This recent one was
in Taipei, in which

01:09:47.939 --> 01:09:50.970
we asked the people
to go in their city

01:09:50.970 --> 01:09:52.500
and bring local materials.

01:09:52.500 --> 01:09:55.260
And we were trying to
understand their relationship

01:09:55.260 --> 01:10:00.750
and their idea about
local materials.

01:10:00.750 --> 01:10:02.350
One minute.

01:10:02.350 --> 01:10:04.020
These are our future plans.

01:10:04.020 --> 01:10:06.180
In the next phase, we
are going to penetrate

01:10:06.180 --> 01:10:09.060
into the underprivileged regions
to reach out to the artists

01:10:09.060 --> 01:10:09.660
there.

01:10:09.660 --> 01:10:12.270
We are going to use eco-friendly
transportation, like e-bikes

01:10:12.270 --> 01:10:15.140
and bicycles, and local
public transportation.

01:10:15.140 --> 01:10:17.340
We are going to partner
with art galleries, museums,

01:10:17.340 --> 01:10:19.630
and art festivals in the
analysis of the world.

01:10:19.630 --> 01:10:21.380
In the Phase 3, we
are going to expand

01:10:21.380 --> 01:10:24.090
into cities which put
environment first, for example,

01:10:24.090 --> 01:10:25.890
Copenhagen and Amsterdam.

01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:27.360
We are going to
launch an auction

01:10:27.360 --> 01:10:29.280
on our website in
which the artist will

01:10:29.280 --> 01:10:31.128
get much more exposure.

01:10:31.128 --> 01:10:33.420
We are going to use technology
to review the artwork so

01:10:33.420 --> 01:10:35.878
that we maintain high-quality
work on our website.

01:10:35.878 --> 01:10:37.920
And finally, we are going
to build an institution

01:10:37.920 --> 01:10:39.720
around the core
idea of UnMake that

01:10:39.720 --> 01:10:42.750
matches art and sustainability.

01:10:42.750 --> 01:10:45.450
So overall, we are
setting the art industry

01:10:45.450 --> 01:10:48.360
as an example in the move
towards sustainability, which

01:10:48.360 --> 01:10:51.450
will inspire other industries
to move towards a sustainable

01:10:51.450 --> 01:10:53.100
future.

01:10:53.100 --> 01:10:53.600
Thank you.

01:10:56.340 --> 01:10:58.380
Thank you so much, Piyush.

01:10:58.380 --> 01:11:00.060
Let's get right
into some questions.

01:11:00.060 --> 01:11:03.350
I see that Josh has his hand up.

01:11:03.350 --> 01:11:06.010
Hi, thank you for
the presentation.

01:11:06.010 --> 01:11:08.590
In viewing your
slides as a customer,

01:11:08.590 --> 01:11:11.770
I want to know a bit more
about the quality I can expect.

01:11:11.770 --> 01:11:13.450
I'm curious to know--

01:11:13.450 --> 01:11:15.885
I didn't see any specific
physical prototypes.

01:11:15.885 --> 01:11:17.260
Could you speak
a little bit more

01:11:17.260 --> 01:11:19.840
about the quality of the
bamboo frames, the brightness

01:11:19.840 --> 01:11:22.540
of the inks, the materials
and what a customer can

01:11:22.540 --> 01:11:24.790
expect on that level?

01:11:24.790 --> 01:11:29.240
So that is explained
by our partnerships,

01:11:29.240 --> 01:11:33.160
and we've been partnering
with several organizations who

01:11:33.160 --> 01:11:39.010
are working with these materials
in India and in the US.

01:11:39.010 --> 01:11:41.620
Right now, we don't have a
physical prototype to show you,

01:11:41.620 --> 01:11:45.940
but our partnerships show that
these people have been working

01:11:45.940 --> 01:11:49.360
on that and we can produce
a high-quality product

01:11:49.360 --> 01:11:53.214
with the help of these
experienced stakeholders.

01:11:56.930 --> 01:11:57.780
All right.

01:11:57.780 --> 01:11:58.470
How about Mark?

01:12:03.420 --> 01:12:07.410
Make of any particular painting?

01:12:07.410 --> 01:12:09.320
Sorry, me?

01:12:09.320 --> 01:12:10.880
Mark, can you
please repeat that?

01:12:10.880 --> 01:12:12.740
Is there is a
limit on the number

01:12:12.740 --> 01:12:16.130
of copies you would make
for a given painting?

01:12:16.130 --> 01:12:18.110
No, it totally
depends on the artist.

01:12:18.110 --> 01:12:19.340
And it's not just paintings.

01:12:19.340 --> 01:12:22.580
It's paintings, photographs,
digital artwork illustrations,

01:12:22.580 --> 01:12:24.750
any kind of visual art.

01:12:24.750 --> 01:12:26.750
So you don't envision--
there would probably not

01:12:26.750 --> 01:12:30.360
be a secondary market for
this work, or there might be?

01:12:30.360 --> 01:12:34.290
Yes, so our business model
actually provides transparency

01:12:34.290 --> 01:12:36.420
and uniqueness in which
the artist is allowed

01:12:36.420 --> 01:12:38.500
to do their own
business on the side

01:12:38.500 --> 01:12:42.150
and our model will actually
enhance their existing business

01:12:42.150 --> 01:12:46.130
by using the
sustainability narrative.

01:12:46.130 --> 01:12:48.005
All right, let's
hear from Wendy next.

01:13:01.780 --> 01:13:02.892
Piyush, how are you?

01:13:02.892 --> 01:13:03.600
Sorry about that.

01:13:03.600 --> 01:13:04.600
Good, how are you Wendy?

01:13:04.600 --> 01:13:06.030
I'm great, I'm great.

01:13:06.030 --> 01:13:06.960
Hey, quick question.

01:13:06.960 --> 01:13:08.916
How are you going
to vet your artists?

01:13:08.916 --> 01:13:10.560
Or are you?

01:13:10.560 --> 01:13:12.030
Sorry, can you come again?

01:13:12.030 --> 01:13:13.890
How will you vet your artists?

01:13:13.890 --> 01:13:16.500
How will you decide
who's the lucky ones who

01:13:16.500 --> 01:13:19.560
gets to upload their artwork?

01:13:19.560 --> 01:13:22.170
Yeah, so as I said, we
have professional artists

01:13:22.170 --> 01:13:25.200
on our team and we will be
doing a careful curation

01:13:25.200 --> 01:13:26.730
of the artwork that we receive.

01:13:26.730 --> 01:13:28.688
And we have already-- if
you go to our website,

01:13:28.688 --> 01:13:30.910
you'll see a bunch of
artworks which we have already

01:13:30.910 --> 01:13:33.390
curated and put on the website
and we took several days

01:13:33.390 --> 01:13:34.920
to choose the best artworks.

01:13:34.920 --> 01:13:37.260
And also we are reading the
background of the artists,

01:13:37.260 --> 01:13:39.060
their experience
in the industry,

01:13:39.060 --> 01:13:42.650
and their relevance
to our product.

01:13:42.650 --> 01:13:43.490
Thank you.

01:13:43.490 --> 01:13:44.660
Wonderful, thank you.

01:13:44.660 --> 01:13:48.660
Let's have a question
from Phillip next, please.

01:13:48.660 --> 01:13:49.830
Hi.

01:13:49.830 --> 01:13:54.870
So I mean, I see your drive
towards sustainability

01:13:54.870 --> 01:13:56.510
as a differentiator.

01:13:56.510 --> 01:14:02.070
The product as I see it, is
a on-demand poster printing

01:14:02.070 --> 01:14:03.660
service with a custom frame.

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:05.800
Is that correct?

01:14:05.800 --> 01:14:07.560
No, that's not actually true.

01:14:07.560 --> 01:14:10.180
If you look at your
value proposition--

01:14:10.180 --> 01:14:12.110
can you see my slide?

01:14:12.110 --> 01:14:12.680
No.

01:14:12.680 --> 01:14:14.660
Can you see my screen?

01:14:14.660 --> 01:14:16.640
You're not screen
sharing because we've

01:14:16.640 --> 01:14:19.990
stopped screen sharing for
the deliberation portion.

01:14:19.990 --> 01:14:22.275
For the Q&amp;A portion.

01:14:22.275 --> 01:14:23.900
So I think that's a
very good question,

01:14:23.900 --> 01:14:25.820
and I would like
to make it clear

01:14:25.820 --> 01:14:28.010
that it's not just a
business of selling art.

01:14:28.010 --> 01:14:31.340
We are trying to connect local
and global artists together

01:14:31.340 --> 01:14:32.420
by our venture.

01:14:32.420 --> 01:14:35.290
So for example, if you're an
artist sitting here in the US,

01:14:35.290 --> 01:14:38.330
you publish your work in our
platform and a person in India

01:14:38.330 --> 01:14:42.800
likes it, they buy it, and
we with our local partners,

01:14:42.800 --> 01:14:45.050
produce it using
sustainable materials

01:14:45.050 --> 01:14:47.350
and with the help of
the local Indian craft,

01:14:47.350 --> 01:14:48.350
we are going to make it.

01:14:48.350 --> 01:14:51.570
So it's a bridge of different
art cultures together.

01:14:51.570 --> 01:14:55.740
There's the primary
value proposition.

01:14:55.740 --> 01:14:59.630
So it's not just that it gives
money or business opportunity

01:14:59.630 --> 01:15:02.180
to the artist, but also
boosts the local economy

01:15:02.180 --> 01:15:02.900
and local craft.

01:15:07.460 --> 01:15:07.960
All right.

01:15:07.960 --> 01:15:08.940
Let's hear from Gui.

01:15:22.600 --> 01:15:23.540
You're muted, Gui.

01:15:25.870 --> 01:15:26.370
Gui?

01:15:29.340 --> 01:15:29.840
Sorry.

01:15:32.430 --> 01:15:35.170
So just expanding on
Philip's question.

01:15:35.170 --> 01:15:37.800
You're going to be a
curator of those artists.

01:15:37.800 --> 01:15:39.540
So artists are
going to be invited

01:15:39.540 --> 01:15:41.760
to participate in
this whole environment

01:15:41.760 --> 01:15:46.050
that you are creating
that produces

01:15:46.050 --> 01:15:48.730
that the copies of the
work and et cetera.

01:15:48.730 --> 01:15:52.230
So you're going to do
like some management

01:15:52.230 --> 01:15:56.460
and curation of the artists,
not just produce the copies?

01:15:56.460 --> 01:15:57.400
Yeah, exactly.

01:15:57.400 --> 01:15:59.170
So we're not just
looking at artworks,

01:15:59.170 --> 01:16:03.270
we're also looking at the
artist and his overall team,

01:16:03.270 --> 01:16:06.210
his works, his
experience, and the kind

01:16:06.210 --> 01:16:08.430
of artwork he produces.

01:16:08.430 --> 01:16:10.020
And then we are
going to also talk

01:16:10.020 --> 01:16:13.080
with our local
partners, our craftsmen,

01:16:13.080 --> 01:16:18.900
if these artworks are relevant
to what they feel, you know,

01:16:18.900 --> 01:16:20.460
what they believe in.

01:16:20.460 --> 01:16:23.220
So it's a partnership with the
local craftsmen, as well as

01:16:23.220 --> 01:16:24.760
the artists.

01:16:24.760 --> 01:16:25.260
OK.

01:16:25.260 --> 01:16:27.840
So just to be clear, like
if I'm not part of this

01:16:27.840 --> 01:16:33.570
and I want to print my own
artwork in sustainable ink

01:16:33.570 --> 01:16:36.490
and frame it sustainably
and all that stuff,

01:16:36.490 --> 01:16:39.880
I'm not going to be allowed--

01:16:39.880 --> 01:16:43.300
you're not going to provide
that service to third parties?

01:16:43.300 --> 01:16:45.980
Just for your own artists?

01:16:45.980 --> 01:16:46.710
No, why not?

01:16:46.710 --> 01:16:48.600
I mean, you can definitely
reach out to us.

01:16:48.600 --> 01:16:50.460
There's a form on our website.

01:16:50.460 --> 01:16:52.970
If you fill that form,
and you upload your work,

01:16:52.970 --> 01:16:55.593
we read your work right away.

01:16:55.593 --> 01:16:57.510
And then we see if you
fulfill our conditions.

01:16:57.510 --> 01:17:00.624
Then we'll definitely put
your work on our website.

01:17:00.624 --> 01:17:01.440
OK.

01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:03.820
So it's transparent and
it's open to everyone.

01:17:03.820 --> 01:17:05.740
The only things we
are we are considering

01:17:05.740 --> 01:17:08.110
is that the artwork should
not incite violence,

01:17:08.110 --> 01:17:10.830
and it should not provide--

01:17:10.830 --> 01:17:13.900
it should not give
anti-sustainable ideas

01:17:13.900 --> 01:17:15.570
to the audience.

01:17:15.570 --> 01:17:16.070
All right.

01:17:16.070 --> 01:17:20.180
Thank you so much, Team UnMake.

01:17:20.180 --> 01:17:22.490
Thank you all for your
wonderful pitches.

01:17:22.490 --> 01:17:24.520
It was really exciting
to hear all of those.

01:17:24.520 --> 01:17:25.430
Thank you!

01:17:25.430 --> 01:17:26.620
Thank you!

01:17:26.620 --> 01:17:28.730
So now, the judges
will be departing

01:17:28.730 --> 01:17:32.150
for approximately 45
minutes of deliberation.

01:17:32.150 --> 01:17:35.150
Now, I invite you to submit
questions to our teams

01:17:35.150 --> 01:17:37.490
and enjoy some conversation
about these pitches

01:17:37.490 --> 01:17:39.380
that you've just heard.

01:17:39.380 --> 01:17:43.220
We'll begin with questions that
were submitted via the Q&amp;A box,

01:17:43.220 --> 01:17:45.290
and then we'll be
allowing attendees

01:17:45.290 --> 01:17:47.960
to be unmuted for the rest
of the conversational portion

01:17:47.960 --> 01:17:51.440
of the webinar, if there
is time for such a thing.

01:17:51.440 --> 01:17:54.500
Following some Q&amp;A, we'll be
holding the Audience Choice

01:17:54.500 --> 01:17:55.220
voting.

01:17:55.220 --> 01:17:57.260
So please cast a
vote to determine

01:17:57.260 --> 01:17:59.690
who will win a $500 prize.

01:17:59.690 --> 01:18:04.430
And then we'll hear an update
from our 2019 15K winning team.

01:18:04.430 --> 01:18:06.950
Mark Adams will be here
from Teach to Learn

01:18:06.950 --> 01:18:09.260
to give you an update about
all the exciting things

01:18:09.260 --> 01:18:12.920
that they've done
since they won the 15K.

01:18:12.920 --> 01:18:16.130
And I hope you enjoy the
conversation, the voting,

01:18:16.130 --> 01:18:19.880
and the update, and we'll
return shortly for our finale

01:18:19.880 --> 01:18:21.740
to reveal this year's winners.

01:18:21.740 --> 01:18:23.090
Thank you all so much.

01:18:23.090 --> 01:18:23.750
See you soon.

01:18:27.770 --> 01:18:28.500
Hi, everyone.

01:18:28.500 --> 01:18:33.167
So the first submitted
question was for ArcLive.

01:18:33.167 --> 01:18:35.750
The question was-- This sounds
like a really cool startup that

01:18:35.750 --> 01:18:37.970
fills a niche, untapped market.

01:18:37.970 --> 01:18:40.070
He's wondering how the
partnerships are going

01:18:40.070 --> 01:18:43.220
and if you have any feedback
on the product so far.

01:18:43.220 --> 01:18:45.080
ArcLive, do you
want to take that?

01:18:45.080 --> 01:18:46.010
Sure.

01:18:46.010 --> 01:18:51.770
Partnerships were going well
until the coronavirus hit.

01:18:51.770 --> 01:18:54.050
It's been rocky over
the past few months

01:18:54.050 --> 01:18:59.030
because a lot of archives have
just completely shut down.

01:18:59.030 --> 01:19:04.710
We've been talking with MIT, the
main MIT archives and the Aga

01:19:04.710 --> 01:19:07.280
Khan Documentation Center here.

01:19:07.280 --> 01:19:09.110
We're looking to
get into Harvard.

01:19:09.110 --> 01:19:12.320
We've been prioritizing
a lot of the--

01:19:12.320 --> 01:19:16.130
specifically in Boston--
a lot of larger ones.

01:19:16.130 --> 01:19:19.393
After we focus on Boston,
we'll probably head to New York

01:19:19.393 --> 01:19:21.560
next just because it's like
a four hour drive there.

01:19:21.560 --> 01:19:22.940
So that's pretty good.

01:19:22.940 --> 01:19:25.700
And then we'll be focusing
all the major cities probably

01:19:25.700 --> 01:19:27.890
on the west coast,
like LA, San Francisco.

01:19:30.560 --> 01:19:31.060
Great.

01:19:31.060 --> 01:19:32.005
Maybe just to--

01:19:32.005 --> 01:19:33.880
Audience members feel
free-- sorry, go ahead.

01:19:33.880 --> 01:19:35.620
Go, Derek.

01:19:35.620 --> 01:19:36.430
Sorry, Susan.

01:19:36.430 --> 01:19:39.880
I was just going to say, we
see a lot of potential, too,

01:19:39.880 --> 01:19:44.020
in the private sector to partner
with private sector enterprises

01:19:44.020 --> 01:19:49.090
specifically with Ancestry.com,
which is a $650 million

01:19:49.090 --> 01:19:50.530
business.

01:19:50.530 --> 01:19:52.120
We see a lot of
opportunity there.

01:19:52.120 --> 01:19:55.010
They have some open
access to public records.

01:19:55.010 --> 01:19:57.850
But when it comes to
actual archival access,

01:19:57.850 --> 01:19:59.410
those are restricted.

01:19:59.410 --> 01:20:01.540
And we see partnership
opportunities with them

01:20:01.540 --> 01:20:06.530
to get our product out there
through our subscription model.

01:20:06.530 --> 01:20:07.990
Great, thank you.

01:20:07.990 --> 01:20:10.490
And just for the audience, feel
free to submit any questions

01:20:10.490 --> 01:20:12.230
into the Q&amp;A or you
can raise your hands

01:20:12.230 --> 01:20:13.800
and we can put you on video.

01:20:13.800 --> 01:20:16.190
I see we just had one come
in for Material Futures Lab.

01:20:16.190 --> 01:20:18.607
Are you considering working
with small artists, designers,

01:20:18.607 --> 01:20:20.850
hand-makers, who are big
supporters of sustainability

01:20:20.850 --> 01:20:21.350
in textiles?

01:20:25.835 --> 01:20:28.470
This background is awful.

01:20:28.470 --> 01:20:30.080
Yeah, I think we definitely are.

01:20:30.080 --> 01:20:33.440
Right now, we're very interested
actually to start producing

01:20:33.440 --> 01:20:35.780
at a large enough
quantity that we

01:20:35.780 --> 01:20:39.950
can be creating enough supply
to reach people like that.

01:20:39.950 --> 01:20:42.270
We're definitely interested
in partnerships as well.

01:20:42.270 --> 01:20:47.380
So if you are interested,
send us an email.

01:20:47.380 --> 01:20:52.010
[CHUCKLING] I have no
idea how Zoom works.

01:20:52.010 --> 01:20:55.910
Yeah, you can send us an email
or get in touch with Shannon

01:20:55.910 --> 01:20:58.730
and just let us know and
we're happy to get in touch.

01:21:03.260 --> 01:21:06.230
So just some general
questions for everybody.

01:21:06.230 --> 01:21:08.870
What has been the most enjoyable
aspect of this competition?

01:21:08.870 --> 01:21:11.150
I know it's been sort of
a long ride this year,

01:21:11.150 --> 01:21:12.750
and a lot of
differences, obviously,

01:21:12.750 --> 01:21:14.327
in the last couple of months.

01:21:14.327 --> 01:21:16.910
What's been the most enjoyable
part of the competition so far?

01:21:23.202 --> 01:21:25.030
Does anyone want to
answer that on this?

01:21:25.030 --> 01:21:27.132
Oh yeah, go for it.

01:21:27.132 --> 01:21:28.840
Yes, I think what was
really interesting,

01:21:28.840 --> 01:21:31.200
particularly
pre-COVID times when

01:21:31.200 --> 01:21:34.260
we're able to all be together,
was the mentorship sessions

01:21:34.260 --> 01:21:36.720
and then also taking that
onto Mentorly digitally.

01:21:36.720 --> 01:21:38.730
But it was a really
interesting way

01:21:38.730 --> 01:21:41.850
of allowing all of the
groups to engage and learn

01:21:41.850 --> 01:21:44.490
from one another and grow
their pitches and business

01:21:44.490 --> 01:21:47.860
development, regardless of
whether you were a finalist

01:21:47.860 --> 01:21:49.120
or not.

01:21:49.120 --> 01:21:51.730
So I thought those opportunities
were fantastic as a learning

01:21:51.730 --> 01:21:54.714
opportunity for all of us.

01:21:54.714 --> 01:21:57.460
Great, thanks.

01:21:57.460 --> 01:22:00.330
Does any else want
to field that one?

01:22:00.330 --> 01:22:01.550
OK, great.

01:22:01.550 --> 01:22:03.090
So a question for UnMake.

01:22:03.090 --> 01:22:05.960
Are artists allowed to make
sustainable copies of artworks

01:22:05.960 --> 01:22:08.480
through UnMake?

01:22:08.480 --> 01:22:09.512
Yes, definitely.

01:22:09.512 --> 01:22:10.970
They're allowed to
make sustainable

01:22:10.970 --> 01:22:12.492
copies of their artworks, yes.

01:22:15.430 --> 01:22:17.430
For P(art)ner, do you
have any partnerships that

01:22:17.430 --> 01:22:19.440
you've already established or
interest that you've seen from

01:22:19.440 --> 01:22:20.430
real estate developers?

01:22:20.430 --> 01:22:23.013
Would you seek out partnerships
from city governments as well?

01:22:27.570 --> 01:22:29.160
Yeah, absolutely.

01:22:29.160 --> 01:22:33.090
We would love to
partner with Boston.

01:22:33.090 --> 01:22:36.810
The City of Boston, they
just started a really awesome

01:22:36.810 --> 01:22:38.580
Percent for Art
Program that they're

01:22:38.580 --> 01:22:40.222
testing out and
piloting right now.

01:22:40.222 --> 01:22:41.930
And also in San
Francisco, because that's

01:22:41.930 --> 01:22:43.440
where Natasha and
I are currently

01:22:43.440 --> 01:22:45.570
based in the two coasts.

01:22:45.570 --> 01:22:49.140
We first met Sebastian,
who's building out

01:22:49.140 --> 01:22:56.050
this really awesome adaptive
reuse community near Davis

01:22:56.050 --> 01:23:00.620
Square, and we just really want
to be aligned with developers

01:23:00.620 --> 01:23:03.940
who are artists themselves or
really care about social impact

01:23:03.940 --> 01:23:05.450
and are very mission-driven.

01:23:05.450 --> 01:23:07.480
So we're really going
after those developers

01:23:07.480 --> 01:23:10.830
and have been in a lot of
great conversations with them.

01:23:10.830 --> 01:23:13.055
Thanks.

01:23:13.055 --> 01:23:15.000
Great.

01:23:15.000 --> 01:23:17.040
Another question for UnMake.

01:23:17.040 --> 01:23:18.750
As an artist, if I
send you something

01:23:18.750 --> 01:23:21.485
depending on what materials
are used at every level,

01:23:21.485 --> 01:23:23.610
the maker's choices will
produce different results,

01:23:23.610 --> 01:23:24.942
some that I might not like.

01:23:24.942 --> 01:23:27.150
Will there be a proofing
process for both the artists

01:23:27.150 --> 01:23:28.930
and the buyers?

01:23:28.930 --> 01:23:29.430
Yes.

01:23:29.430 --> 01:23:32.550
So, our process involves
the collaboration

01:23:32.550 --> 01:23:34.500
of both the
participating artists,

01:23:34.500 --> 01:23:35.940
as well as the craftsmen.

01:23:35.940 --> 01:23:38.280
And whatever
material we produce,

01:23:38.280 --> 01:23:40.320
whatever the final
product is, it

01:23:40.320 --> 01:23:44.040
involves both the artist,
as well as the craftsman.

01:23:44.040 --> 01:23:46.300
And if the artist does
not want a particular kind

01:23:46.300 --> 01:23:48.460
of craft attached
to their artwork,

01:23:48.460 --> 01:23:52.005
they are free to tell us that.

01:23:52.005 --> 01:23:54.502
Great, thank you.

01:23:54.502 --> 01:23:56.960
What's been the most important
lesson that any of the teams

01:23:56.960 --> 01:23:58.550
have learned
throughout the process?

01:23:58.550 --> 01:24:00.800
I know for many of you it's
been a very long process

01:24:00.800 --> 01:24:02.467
and some shorter, but
are there any sort

01:24:02.467 --> 01:24:05.720
of major landmarks that felt
like big discoveries learned?

01:24:12.900 --> 01:24:14.000
Tricky question, I'm sure.

01:24:16.567 --> 01:24:18.150
Sorry, Rarify, did
you have an answer?

01:24:18.150 --> 01:24:18.730
Go for it.

01:24:18.730 --> 01:24:24.392
Yeah, I mean, I think this was
a great opportunity to really

01:24:24.392 --> 01:24:26.100
dive into, for instance,
a business plan.

01:24:26.100 --> 01:24:31.920
And so having the time and
accountability to devote

01:24:31.920 --> 01:24:34.500
to looking at a business
plan and seeing critiques,

01:24:34.500 --> 01:24:40.890
seeing how relevant and acute
the feedback was from those

01:24:40.890 --> 01:24:42.845
that we receive feedback from.

01:24:42.845 --> 01:24:44.220
And so you know,
it's always it's

01:24:44.220 --> 01:24:45.480
interesting when
you're speculating,

01:24:45.480 --> 01:24:47.620
OK, what's your revenue going
to be, and all this stuff.

01:24:47.620 --> 01:24:49.578
But at least being able
to have the opportunity

01:24:49.578 --> 01:24:51.900
to look through,
investigate, speculate,

01:24:51.900 --> 01:24:56.660
and try to be as accurate
and rigorous as possible.

01:24:56.660 --> 01:24:57.160
Great.

01:24:57.160 --> 01:24:58.520
Thank you.

01:24:58.520 --> 01:25:00.393
A question came
in for P(art)ner.

01:25:00.393 --> 01:25:01.810
Do you have any
idea of the number

01:25:01.810 --> 01:25:03.580
of local artists
capable or eager

01:25:03.580 --> 01:25:05.050
to work at the public art scale?

01:25:09.190 --> 01:25:13.570
The idea here is that
some folks might not

01:25:13.570 --> 01:25:16.000
realize that they're
perfect for public art.

01:25:16.000 --> 01:25:20.650
Maybe they work with fabric,
or other materials that

01:25:20.650 --> 01:25:24.490
aren't 2D or 3D in the same
way that large-scale art is,

01:25:24.490 --> 01:25:26.508
but that they've
never really realized

01:25:26.508 --> 01:25:27.550
their potential that way.

01:25:27.550 --> 01:25:31.210
And so because we're really
leaning on reaching out

01:25:31.210 --> 01:25:35.470
to non-profit arts organizations
that have a lot of artists

01:25:35.470 --> 01:25:37.000
and residents,
we're really hoping

01:25:37.000 --> 01:25:42.040
that folks at these non-profits
can kind of be curating

01:25:42.040 --> 01:25:45.220
and introducing artists
to these new ideas

01:25:45.220 --> 01:25:50.380
because they really know
their artists pools well.

01:25:50.380 --> 01:25:52.460
Are you picturing
having opportunities

01:25:52.460 --> 01:25:55.210
for both physically
smaller-scale public art, as

01:25:55.210 --> 01:25:57.790
opposed to just gigantic
murals and that kind of thing?

01:25:57.790 --> 01:25:59.770
I think that's one
of the questions,

01:25:59.770 --> 01:26:01.990
is that if you're not a
muralist or a sculptor,

01:26:01.990 --> 01:26:03.690
is there an opportunity there?

01:26:03.690 --> 01:26:05.770
I think that we want to--

01:26:05.770 --> 01:26:07.540
No, go ahead.

01:26:07.540 --> 01:26:12.670
We definitely want to address
the fact that art doesn't have

01:26:12.670 --> 01:26:15.250
to just be murals, especially
for the public art realm,

01:26:15.250 --> 01:26:16.750
it's really interesting
to hear what

01:26:16.750 --> 01:26:18.000
the different regulations are.

01:26:18.000 --> 01:26:21.340
For example, in
Massachusetts, public art

01:26:21.340 --> 01:26:23.710
can only be sculptural in 3D.

01:26:23.710 --> 01:26:25.840
And then in
California, public art

01:26:25.840 --> 01:26:30.310
can be extended towards live
performances, and exhibitions,

01:26:30.310 --> 01:26:31.990
and word speech.

01:26:31.990 --> 01:26:35.860
So we really want to
expand on what art is.

01:26:35.860 --> 01:26:38.860
And then in terms of
the previous question,

01:26:38.860 --> 01:26:41.560
in terms of the
number of artists,

01:26:41.560 --> 01:26:43.130
as part of our
business plan we want

01:26:43.130 --> 01:26:48.240
to create a curated experience
so that every single city

01:26:48.240 --> 01:26:51.750
we would only be representing
about 60 artists.

01:26:51.750 --> 01:26:54.880
So we do want to be mindful
about diluting the pool.

01:26:57.550 --> 01:26:59.240
Great, thank you.

01:26:59.240 --> 01:27:01.830
A couple more
questions for UnMake.

01:27:01.830 --> 01:27:03.370
One, just how the
name came about.

01:27:03.370 --> 01:27:05.680
And also, if you have
a economic breakdown

01:27:05.680 --> 01:27:08.005
for each section of the process.

01:27:10.940 --> 01:27:15.160
So right now, as I
explained in the slide,

01:27:15.160 --> 01:27:19.660
that we have calculated
the production cost based

01:27:19.660 --> 01:27:27.280
on the material price plus
the price of the construction

01:27:27.280 --> 01:27:30.340
that includes the labor cost,
as well as the cost of printing.

01:27:30.340 --> 01:27:32.740
And in New York,
it's around $58,

01:27:32.740 --> 01:27:34.598
and in Bombay it's around $25.

01:27:34.598 --> 01:27:35.890
Does that answer your question?

01:27:38.630 --> 01:27:42.240
We'll see if the Question
Answer writes back.

01:27:42.240 --> 01:27:44.620
And how did the name come about?

01:27:44.620 --> 01:27:46.300
Yes, the name.

01:27:46.300 --> 01:27:48.690
It basically comes
from the process

01:27:48.690 --> 01:27:54.450
of using recycled materials
because everything that is made

01:27:54.450 --> 01:27:56.003
creates a carbon footprint.

01:27:56.003 --> 01:27:58.170
And since we are only using
recycled and sustainable

01:27:58.170 --> 01:28:00.430
materials, we are
unmaking that process.

01:28:00.430 --> 01:28:02.560
That's why we are called UnMake.

01:28:02.560 --> 01:28:03.060
Great.

01:28:03.060 --> 01:28:05.047
Thank you so much.

01:28:05.047 --> 01:28:06.630
I think one of the
most valuable parts

01:28:06.630 --> 01:28:09.570
from an outside perspective
for this competition

01:28:09.570 --> 01:28:11.610
is the great access
that you all have

01:28:11.610 --> 01:28:14.220
to a really wide
variety of mentors

01:28:14.220 --> 01:28:17.520
and some of the judges
and that feedback process.

01:28:17.520 --> 01:28:19.380
What's been some of
the most useful advice

01:28:19.380 --> 01:28:21.600
that you've gotten from
mentors throughout this?

01:28:24.750 --> 01:28:27.790
ArcLive, do you want
to answer that one?

01:28:27.790 --> 01:28:28.290
Yes, sure.

01:28:28.290 --> 01:28:29.270
I'm happy to.

01:28:29.270 --> 01:28:31.370
Yeah, the Mentorly
resources were great.

01:28:31.370 --> 01:28:34.430
I think they really
forced us to ask

01:28:34.430 --> 01:28:37.850
how we can leverage our
technology to also gain access

01:28:37.850 --> 01:28:40.790
to copyrighted material so
that we're not simply putting

01:28:40.790 --> 01:28:43.970
in workstations, but
actually gaining access

01:28:43.970 --> 01:28:45.980
to those copyrights.

01:28:45.980 --> 01:28:49.010
There's a lot of
potential future revenue

01:28:49.010 --> 01:28:50.900
that could be derived
if we can do so.

01:28:50.900 --> 01:28:53.120
So that was certainly
something that's

01:28:53.120 --> 01:28:55.760
forced us to rethink a
bit how we move forward.

01:29:01.702 --> 01:29:04.160
Did any of them the other teams
have really useful feedback

01:29:04.160 --> 01:29:07.403
that helped shape how their
presentations have developed

01:29:07.403 --> 01:29:08.570
over the course of the year?

01:29:12.250 --> 01:29:14.500
Sure, I can respond
to that quickly.

01:29:14.500 --> 01:29:18.022
Both Maria and I, we don't
come from business backgrounds.

01:29:18.022 --> 01:29:19.230
We're architects by training.

01:29:19.230 --> 01:29:21.360
So in a sense, this
was our first time

01:29:21.360 --> 01:29:23.220
trying to develop
a business plan

01:29:23.220 --> 01:29:25.450
and all the things that
are associated with that.

01:29:25.450 --> 01:29:29.790
So I would say the
mentors really lasered in

01:29:29.790 --> 01:29:33.120
on all of our blind spots
and showed us the questions

01:29:33.120 --> 01:29:35.940
we didn't even know to ask,
so that by the end of this

01:29:35.940 --> 01:29:37.740
we were able to kind
of sharpen the idea

01:29:37.740 --> 01:29:43.275
and get some where hopefully
somewhat convincing.

01:29:43.275 --> 01:29:45.020
That's great, thank you.

01:29:45.020 --> 01:29:48.040
Rarify, had you wanted
to answer that one?

01:29:48.040 --> 01:29:50.160
Sure, I was just
going to say that it's

01:29:50.160 --> 01:29:52.710
been really nice to work
through with our mentors

01:29:52.710 --> 01:29:57.360
and with other peers, the story
that we're telling and making

01:29:57.360 --> 01:30:00.270
sure that our
passion comes across

01:30:00.270 --> 01:30:03.670
because our venture
is so personal to us

01:30:03.670 --> 01:30:05.730
as everyone's is.

01:30:05.730 --> 01:30:07.810
So really, telling a
story has been something

01:30:07.810 --> 01:30:11.730
that we've loved working on.

01:30:11.730 --> 01:30:12.230
Fantastic.

01:30:12.230 --> 01:30:13.790
Thank you so much.

01:30:13.790 --> 01:30:15.622
One question came in
for Material Futures.

01:30:15.622 --> 01:30:17.330
What sort of performance
testing have you

01:30:17.330 --> 01:30:19.667
performed on your
biologically-produced pigments?

01:30:19.667 --> 01:30:20.750
Do they bleach in the sun?

01:30:20.750 --> 01:30:24.060
Will they withstand washing
at high temperatures?

01:30:24.060 --> 01:30:24.560
Et cetera.

01:30:26.950 --> 01:30:27.450
Sorry--

01:30:27.450 --> 01:30:32.070
It's performance-- Huh?

01:30:32.070 --> 01:30:32.570
No, sorry.

01:30:32.570 --> 01:30:35.900
I think we just typed an answer.

01:30:35.900 --> 01:30:37.200
OK, great.

01:30:37.200 --> 01:30:40.288
You beat me to it.

01:30:40.288 --> 01:30:40.830
That's great.

01:30:40.830 --> 01:30:42.290
Awesome.

01:30:42.290 --> 01:30:44.090
Are there any
differences between

01:30:44.090 --> 01:30:46.400
the performance and
utility of these pigments

01:30:46.400 --> 01:30:49.100
and other commercially,
industrial made ones

01:30:49.100 --> 01:30:50.910
that you've found so far?

01:30:50.910 --> 01:30:52.220
Yeah, we can talk about that.

01:30:52.220 --> 01:30:54.260
So of course synthetic
pigments they're

01:30:54.260 --> 01:30:58.070
made often with
metals, heavy metals

01:30:58.070 --> 01:31:00.380
and often have very,
very strong fastness.

01:31:00.380 --> 01:31:02.380
That's kind of what they've
been designed to do,

01:31:02.380 --> 01:31:06.110
or specific
performance attributes.

01:31:06.110 --> 01:31:08.300
And so our pigments
are organic pigments.

01:31:08.300 --> 01:31:10.277
So by nature,
they're biodegradable

01:31:10.277 --> 01:31:11.360
and they're fully organic.

01:31:11.360 --> 01:31:15.740
So their lifespan is likely
not going to be as long.

01:31:15.740 --> 01:31:18.560
We have had really good
results for life fastness

01:31:18.560 --> 01:31:22.740
and washing up to temperatures
of 120 degrees so far,

01:31:22.740 --> 01:31:25.550
but we're going to
be rigorously testing

01:31:25.550 --> 01:31:27.710
over the next couple of
months to really determine

01:31:27.710 --> 01:31:30.290
exactly what specs we have.

01:31:30.290 --> 01:31:32.530
So yeah, hopefully we'll
be able to determine that.

01:31:32.530 --> 01:31:33.030
Great.

01:31:33.030 --> 01:31:35.730
Well I imagine with lululemon
you'll get a pretty good crash

01:31:35.730 --> 01:31:38.250
course in how durable
they are for extreme use.

01:31:38.250 --> 01:31:38.750
[CHUCKLING]

01:31:38.750 --> 01:31:41.250
[CHUCKLING] For sure.

01:31:41.250 --> 01:31:42.510
One more question for UnMake.

01:31:42.510 --> 01:31:44.220
How do you foresee
the art industry

01:31:44.220 --> 01:31:46.490
setting the
sustainability narrative?

01:31:50.750 --> 01:31:52.500
I'm not sure if I
understood the question,

01:31:52.500 --> 01:31:56.760
but what I understood
from that I can answer.

01:31:56.760 --> 01:32:01.890
We can see that the art
industry right now is basically

01:32:01.890 --> 01:32:02.790
quite fragmented.

01:32:02.790 --> 01:32:06.540
There is and there's a whole
sustainability area in the art

01:32:06.540 --> 01:32:10.230
industry which has not
yet been leveraged enough,

01:32:10.230 --> 01:32:12.210
and the artists
really want to work

01:32:12.210 --> 01:32:13.750
with sustainable materials.

01:32:13.750 --> 01:32:16.290
That we got to know
with our interviews

01:32:16.290 --> 01:32:17.830
and also with the research.

01:32:17.830 --> 01:32:20.190
And if we let them use
the sustainable materials

01:32:20.190 --> 01:32:23.725
and also make profit
out of it, that's

01:32:23.725 --> 01:32:25.350
going to make a change
in the industry.

01:32:28.010 --> 01:32:29.250
Great.

01:32:29.250 --> 01:32:30.540
Thank you so much.

